        [Turn 7 REASONING] (2955 chars)
        \[
\textbf{Answer: } n=9
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Normalising the labelling  

Let \(a_{i,j}\;(1\le i,j\le n)\) be the number of coins in the square \((i,j)\).
The condition  

\[
|a_{i,j}-a_{i+1,j}|\le 1,\qquad |a_{i,j}-a_{i,j+1}|\le 1
\tag{1}
\]

holds for every pair of adjacent squares.  
If we subtract the smallest entry \(\min a_{i,j}=m\) from all squares we obtain a new
labelling  

\[
b_{i,j}=a_{i,j}-m\ge 0 ,
\]

which still satisfies (1).  Hence we may (and shall) assume that at least one
square contains **0** coins.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  An upper bound for the total number of coins  

Let a square \(Z\) contain \(0\) coins.  
From (1) a neighbour of \(Z\) can contain at most \(1\) coin, a neighbour of a
neighbour at most \(2\) coins, etc.  Consequently for every square \(S\)

\[
b_{S}\le d(S,Z),
\]

where \(d(S,Z)\) is the Manhattan distance from \(S\) to the zero‑square.
Summing over the whole board gives

\[
\sum_{i,j} b_{i,j}\le \sum_{i,j} d\bigl((i,j),Z\bigr).
\tag{2}
\]

To make the right–hand side as large as possible we place the zero‑square in a
corner; then the distance of \((i,j)\) from that corner is \(i+j\) (indices
starting with \(0\)).  Hence

\[
\sum_{i,j} d\bigl((i,j),\text{corner}\bigr)
   =\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(i+j)
   =n^{2}(n-1).
\tag{3}
\]

Thus any admissible placement on an \(n\times n\) board satisfies  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \sum a_{i,j}\le n^{2}(n-1)} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  The bound is sharp  

The labelling  

\[
a_{i,j}=i+j\qquad(0\le i,j\le n-1)
\tag{4}
\]

places a zero in the upper‑left corner, and the differences of adjacent
squares are exactly \(1\).  Its total number of coins is precisely the right
hand side of (3), i.e. \(n^{2}(n-1)\).  Hence the bound (3) cannot be
improved.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Minimal board size for 471 coins  

From (3) we need  

\[
n^{2}(n-1)\ge 471 .
\]

Checking successive integers:

\[
\begin{aligned}
n=8 &:\; 8^{2}\cdot7 = 448 < 471,\\[2mm]
n=9 &:\; 9^{2}\cdot8 = 648 \ge 471 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore an \(8\times8\) board is insufficient, while a \(9\times9\) board
can accommodate 471 coins.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Obtaining exactly 471 coins on a \(9\times9\) board  

The construction (4) for \(n=9\) gives a total of \(648\) coins.
To reach the required total we simply lower the contents of any
\(648-471=177\) squares by one coin each (never below zero).  
Because decreasing a square by one does not increase any difference beyond
\(1\), the adjacency condition remains satisfied.  Hence a concrete
placement with total \(471\) exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\max}=9\,}
\]

