        \[
\text{Maximum }n=3
\]

**Reason.**  
Let the board be indexed by \((i,j)\;(0\le i,j\le n-1)\) and let  
\(f(i,j)\) be the number of coins in the square \((i,j)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Structure of a feasible labelling  

Choose the left‑bottom cell \((0,0)\) and put  

\[
C=f(0,0),\qquad a_i=f(i,0)-C,\qquad b_j=f(0,j)-C .
\]

Because adjacent cells differ by exactly \(1\),

\[
|f(i+1,j)-f(i,j)|=|a_{i+1}-a_i|=1 ,\qquad
|f(i,j+1)-f(i,j)|=|b_{j+1}-b_j|=1 .
\]

Hence  

\[
a_{i+1}=a_i\pm1,\qquad b_{j+1}=b_j\pm1 .
\]

Using the condition on a unit square,
\(f(i+1,j+1)-f(i+1,j)=f(i,j+1)-f(i,j)\), one checks inductively that  

\[
\boxed{\,f(i,j)=C+a_i+b_j\;}\qquad (0\le i,j\le n-1) .
\]

Thus every admissible configuration is determined by a constant \(C\) and two
integer sequences \((a_i)\) and \((b_j)\) with  

\[
a_0=b_0=0,\qquad |a_{i+1}-a_i|=|b_{j+1}-b_j|=1 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Consequences for the total number of coins  

Summing the formula over all squares gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
S&=\sum_{i,j}f(i,j)\\
  &=n^{2}C+n\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}a_i+n\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}b_j .
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
\]

Hence \(S\) is a multiple of \(n\); in particular  

\[
\boxed{n\mid S}.
\]

Moreover the board is bipartite, so adjacent cells have opposite parity.
If \(n\) were even, each pair of opposite‑coloured cells would contribute an
even number to the total, forcing \(S\) to be even.  
Since \(S=471\) is odd, **\(n\) must be odd**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Possible values of \(n\)

The odd divisors of \(471=3\cdot157\) are  

\[
n\in\{1,3,157,471\}.
\]

For a board of size \(n\) the smallest possible total sum is obtained by
choosing the “most decreasing’’ sequences, namely \(a_i=i\) and \(b_j=-j\) and
\(C=0\).  This gives  

\[
S_{\min}=n(n-1)\qquad (n\text{ odd}),\qquad
S_{\min}=n^{2}\qquad (n\text{ even}).
\]

Thus for \(n\ge 157\),

\[
S_{\min}=n(n-1)\ge 157\cdot156=24492>471,
\]

so such large \(n\) are impossible.  Consequently the only admissible odd
divisors not exceeding this bound are \(n=1\) and \(n=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  A construction for \(n=3\)

Take  

\[
a_0=0,\;a_1=1,\;a_2=0,\qquad
b_0=0,\;b_1=-1,\;b_2=-2,\qquad C=53 .
\]

Then \(f(i,j)=C+a_i+b_j\) yields the board  

\[
\begin{array}{ccc}
53&52&51\\
54&53&52\\
53&52&51
\end{array}
\]

Every pair of adjacent squares differs by exactly \(1\), all entries are
non‑negative, and the total number of coins is  

\[
53+52+51+54+53+52+53+52+51=471 .
\]

Hence an arrangement exists for \(n=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Conclusion  

The only possible board sizes are \(n=1\) and \(n=3\); the larger one is
\(n=3\).

\[
\boxed{\,\displaystyle n_{\max}=3\,}
\]

