Any spanning tree (hence any Hamiltonian path) must contain at least one
edge crossing this cut; such an edge is a 10‑th‑dimension edge.
Removing that edge leaves two copies of \(Q_{10}\); each of them must be
spanned by a tree.

Applying the same argument recursively yields the following
induction:

*For the \((n\!-\!1)\)-dimensional hypercube \(Q_{n}\) any spanning tree
contains at least \(2^{\,n-1-k}\) edges of dimension \(k\)
(\(0\le k\le n-1\)).*

Proof.  
For \(n=1\) the claim is obvious.  
Assume it true for \(Q_{n-1}\).  In a spanning tree of \(Q_n\) there is at
least one edge of dimension \(n-1\); after deleting it we obtain two
spanning trees of the two \((n-1)\)-dimensional subcubes.  By the induction
hypothesis each subcube contributes at least \(2^{(n-2)-k}\) edges of
dimension \(k\); together this gives at least \(2\cdot 2^{(n-2)-k}
=2^{\,n-1-k}\) edges of dimension \(k\) in the whole tree. ∎

