### 1.  Linear‑algebraic formulation  

Put  

\[
b_j=a_j+a_{j+1}\qquad (j=1,\dots ,n),\qquad a_{n+1}=a_1 .
\]

Let  

\[
S=\begin{pmatrix}
1&1&0&\cdots &0\\
0&1&1&\ddots &\vdots\\
\vdots&\ddots&\ddots&\ddots&0\\
0&\cdots&0&1&1\\
1&0&\cdots&0&1
\end{pmatrix}\;(n\times n).
\]

Then \(b=Sa\).  
Since \(n\) is odd, \(S\) is invertible (its eigenvalues are
\(1+\omega_k\) with \(\omega_k=e^{2\pi ik/n}\neq-1\)).
Hence  

\[
a=S^{-1}b .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The inverse of \(S\)

Write \(P\) for the cyclic shift matrix \((P\,x)_j=x_{j+1}\).
Then \(S=I+P\) and \(P^{\,n}=I\).

Because \(n\) is odd,
\[
(I+P)\Bigl(\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^kP^{k}\Bigr)
   =\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^kP^{k}
    +\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^kP^{k+1}
   =I-(-1)^nI=2I .
\]

Thus  

\[
S^{-1}= \frac12\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^kP^{k}.
\]

Consequently each component of \(a\) is a linear combination of the
\(b\)’s with coefficients \(\frac12(-1)^k\):

\[
a_i=\frac12\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^k\,b_{i+k}\qquad (i=1,\dots ,n),
\]
indices being taken modulo \(n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Upper bound for \(\displaystyle\frac{\max|a_i|}{\max|b_j|}\)

From the formula above,

\[
|a_i|
   \le\frac12\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}|b_{i+k}|
   \le\frac12\;n\;\max_{j}|b_j|.
\]

Hence for every vector \((a_1,\dots ,a_n)\),

\[
\max_{i}|a_i|\le\frac{n}{2}\,\max_{j}|a_j+a_{j+1}|.
\tag{1}
\]

For our problem \(n=4467\), so \(\dfrac{n}{2}=2233.5\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Sharpness of the bound  

Take the vector  

\[
b_j=(-1)^j\qquad(j=1,\dots ,n),
\]
so \(\max_j|b_j|=1\).
Using the expression of \(S^{-1}\),

\[
a_i=\frac12\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^k(-1)^{i+k}
     =\frac12\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^{i}= \frac{n}{2}\,(-1)^i .
\]

Thus \(\max_i|a_i|=n/2\) and the ratio
\(\displaystyle\frac{\max|a_i|}{\max|b_j|}=n/2\) is attained.
Therefore the constant \(\frac{n}{2}\) cannot be improved.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Minimal integer \(t\)

The supremum of the ratio is exactly \(\dfrac{n}{2}=2233.5\).
To make the inequality hold for **all** real vectors, \(t\) must be at
least the smallest integer not less than this supremum:

\[
t_{\min}= \bigl\lceil \tfrac{n}{2}\bigr\rceil
        =\bigl\lceil 2233.5\bigr\rceil = 2234 .
\]

