--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Linear‑algebraic form  

Put  

\[
B=\begin{pmatrix}
1&1&0&\dots &0\\
0&1&1&\dots &0\\
\vdots& &\ddots&\ddots&\vdots\\
0&\dots &0&1&1\\
1&0&\dots &0&1
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad 
B\mathbf a=
\begin{pmatrix}
a_1+a_2\\ a_2+a_3\\ \vdots\\ a_{n-1}+a_n\\ a_n+a_1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

The required inequality is  

\[
\|\mathbf a\|_\infty\le t\;\|B\mathbf a\|_\infty .
\]

Hence  

\[
t\;=\;\Big\lceil\sup_{\mathbf a\neq0}
\frac{\|\mathbf a\|_\infty}{\|B\mathbf a\|_\infty}\Big\rceil
   =\Big\lceil\|B^{-1}\|_{\infty\to\infty}\Big\rceil .
\]

The induced \(\infty\)-norm of a matrix \(M\) is  

\[
\|M\|_{\infty\to\infty}= \max_{i}\sum_{j}|M_{ij}|
\qquad(\text{row‑sum norm}).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Inverting \(B\) for odd \(n\)

Let \(S\) be the cyclic shift matrix \((S\mathbf a)_i=a_{i+1}\) (indices
mod \(n\)).  
Then \(B=I+S\) and \(S^{\,n}=I\).

If \(n\) is **odd** we have  

\[
(I+S)\Bigl(I-S+S^{2}-\dots+(-1)^{\,n-1}S^{\,n-1}\Bigr)
   = I-(-S)^{\,n}=I-(-1)^{\,n}I =2I .
\]

Therefore  

\[
B^{-1}= \frac12\Bigl(I-S+S^{2}-\dots+(-1)^{\,n-1}S^{\,n-1}\Bigr)
      =\frac12\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(-1)^{k}S^{k}. \tag{1}
\]

Consequences of (1):

* Every entry of \(B^{-1}\) has absolute value \(\frac12\):
  \[
  (B^{-1})_{ij}= \frac12(-1)^{\,j-i\pmod n}.
  \]

* Each row contains \(n\) such entries, hence the row‑sum of absolute
  values is  

  \[
  \sum_{j=1}^{n}\bigl| (B^{-1})_{ij}\bigr| = n\cdot\frac12 = \frac n2 .
  \]

Thus  

\[
\|B^{-1}\|_{\infty\to\infty}= \frac n2 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The supremum is attained

Take the vector \(\mathbf x\) with components \(x_j=(-1)^{j}\) (\(j=1,\dots,n\)).
From (1),

\[
(B^{-1}\mathbf x)_i
   =\frac12\sum_{j=1}^{n}(-1)^{j-i}\,(-1)^{j}
   =\frac12\sum_{j=1}^{n}(-1)^{2j-i}
   =\frac n2\,(-1)^{i}.
\]

Hence \(\|B^{-1}\mathbf x\|_\infty =\frac n2\) while
\(\| \mathbf x\|_\infty =1\); consequently

\[
\sup_{\mathbf a\neq0}\frac{\|\mathbf a\|_\infty}{\|B\mathbf a\|_\infty}
      =\frac n2 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Even \(n\)

If \(n\) is even, \(\omega^{n/2}=-1\) is an eigenvalue of \(S\) and
\(1+\omega^{n/2}=0\); thus \(B\) is singular.  
There exist non‑zero \(\mathbf a\) with \(B\mathbf a=0\), making the
right‑hand side of the required inequality zero.  
Hence no finite \(t\) works for even \(n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Applying to \(n=4467\)

\(n=4467\) is odd, so the supremum equals \(\dfrac{n}{2}=2233.5\).
The smallest integer \(t\) satisfying the inequality for all real
\((a_1,\dots ,a_{4467})\) is therefore

\[
\boxed{t= \big\lceil\frac{4467}{2}\big\rceil = 2234 } .
\]

The bound is sharp, as shown by the vector \(\mathbf a = B^{-1}\mathbf
x\) with \(x_j=(-1)^{j}\), for which  

\[
\max_i|a_i| = \frac{4467}{2},\qquad
\max_i|a_i+a_{i+1}| = 1,
\]

giving the ratio \(\frac{4467}{2}=2233.5\).  Hence any integer \(t<2234\)
fails, and \(t=2234\) works for every choice of the real numbers.

