        [Turn 4 REASONING] (2608 chars)
        Let  

\[
M(a)=\max_{1\le i\le n}|a_i| ,\qquad 
S(a)=\max_{1\le i\le n}|a_i+a_{i+1}|
\]

with the cyclic convention \(a_{n+1}=a_1\) and \(n=4467\).
Define the linear operator  

\[
T:\mathbb R^{n}\longrightarrow\mathbb R^{n},\qquad (Ta)_i=a_i+a_{i+1}.
\]

The required inequality is  

\[
M(a)\le t\,S(a)=t\|Ta\|_{\infty}\qquad\forall a\in\mathbb R^{n}.
\]

Hence the smallest admissible constant is  

\[
t_{\min}= \sup_{a\neq0}\frac{\|a\|_{\infty}}{\|Ta\|_{\infty}}
        =\|T^{-1}\|_{\infty},
\]

the induced \(\ell_{\infty}\)-norm of the inverse matrix \(T^{-1}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Inverse of \(T\)

\(T\) is the circulant matrix whose first row is  

\[
(1,1,0,\dots ,0).
\]

For a circulant matrix the inverse is also circulant.  
Let the first row of \(T^{-1}\) be \((c_0,c_1,\dots ,c_{n-1})\).
The convolution condition \( (1,1,0,\dots,0)\star(c_0,\dots ,c_{n-1})=e_0\) gives  

\[
c_k+c_{k-1}= \begin{cases}
1,&k\equiv0\pmod n,\\[2pt]
0,&k\not\equiv0\pmod n .
\end{cases}
\]

For \(k\neq0\) we have \(c_k=-c_{k-1}\), whence  

\[
c_k=(-1)^k c_0\qquad(k=0,\dots ,n-1).
\]

Because \(n\) is odd, \(c_{n-1}=c_0\); the equation for \(k=0\) yields  

\[
c_0+c_{n-1}=2c_0=1\Longrightarrow c_0=\frac12 .
\]

Thus  

\[
c_k=\frac12(-1)^k,\qquad k=0,\dots ,n-1 .
\]

Consequently every row of \(T^{-1}\) consists of the numbers \(\pm\frac12\) alternating in sign.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  \(\ell_{\infty}\)-norm of \(T^{-1}\)

For any row the sum of absolute values is  

\[
\sum_{j=1}^{n}\Bigl|\,\frac12(-1)^{j-i}\Bigr| =\frac12\cdot n .
\]

All rows have the same sum, therefore  

\[
\|T^{-1}\|_{\infty}= \frac{n}{2}= \frac{4467}{2}=2233.5 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Minimal integer \(t\)

Since the inequality must hold for all vectors, we need  

\[
t\ge \|T^{-1}\|_{\infty}=2233.5 .
\]

Hence the smallest integer satisfying the condition is  

\[
\boxed{t=2234}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Sharpness  

Take \(b_i=(-1)^i\;(i=1,\dots ,n)\) and set \(a=T^{-1}b\).  
Because each row of \(T^{-1}\) sums the signs of \(b\),

\[
a_i =\frac12\sum_{j=1}^{n}(-1)^{j-i}(-1)^j
     =\frac12\sum_{j=1}^{n}(-1)^{i}= \frac{n}{2}(-1)^i .
\]

Thus  

\[
\max_i|a_i|=\frac{n}{2}=2233.5,\qquad 
\max_i|a_i+a_{i+1}|=1 .
\]

The ratio \(\max|a_i|/\max|a_i+a_{i+1}| = 2233.5\) exceeds any integer \(t\le 2233\); therefore \(t=2233\) does **not** work, confirming that \(2234\) is indeed minimal.

