        \[
\text{Let }n=4467,\qquad 
M=\max_{1\le i\le n}|a_i+a_{i+1}|\;(a_{n+1}=a_1).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An upper bound

For each odd index \(i\) we have  

\[
a_i+a_{i+1}\le M ,
\]

and for each even index \(i\)

\[
a_i+a_{i+1}\ge -M\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad -(a_i+a_{i+1})\le M .
\]

Add all these \(n\) inequalities with the signs \((+,-,+,-,\dots )\):

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{\,i+1}(a_i+a_{i+1})\le nM .
\tag{1}
\]

Because the indices are taken modulo \(n\), each term \(a_k\) appears twice:
once in the summand with index \(i=k\) and once in the summand with
index \(i=k-1\).  
Since \(n\) is odd, the two signs are the same for \(k=1\) and opposite
for every other \(k\).  Consequently the left–hand side of (1) reduces
to

\[
2a_1\le nM .
\]

Thus  

\[
a_1\le \frac{n}{2}\,M .
\]

The same argument applied after a cyclic shift gives the same bound for
every \(a_i\); hence

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\max_{1\le i\le n}|a_i|\le\frac{n}{2}\,
\max_{1\le i\le n}|a_i+a_{i+1}| } .
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Sharpness of the bound

Define a sequence \((a_i)_{i=1}^{n}\) (with \(n\) odd) by  

\[
a_i=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\frac{n}{2}-(i-1), & i\ \text{odd},\\[4pt]
\displaystyle (i-1)-\frac{n}{2}, & i\ \text{even}.
\end{cases}
\]

Then for every \(i\)

\[
a_i+a_{i+1}= 
\begin{cases}
\;1, & i\ \text{odd},\\[2pt]
-1, & i\ \text{even},
\end{cases}
\]

so  

\[
\max_{i}|a_i+a_{i+1}|=1 .
\]

The largest absolute value among the \(a_i\) is attained at \(i=1\):

\[
\max_i|a_i|=a_1=\frac{n}{2}.
\]

Hence the ratio \(\displaystyle\frac{\max_i|a_i|}{\max_i|a_i+a_{i+1}|}\)
equals \(\frac{n}{2}\).  Therefore the constant \(\frac{n}{2}\) in (2) is
best possible.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The required integer \(t\)

For \(n=4467\),

\[
\frac{n}{2}= \frac{4467}{2}=2233.5 .
\]

The inequality must hold for every real vector, so the smallest integer
\(t\) satisfying it is the ceiling of this value:

\[
\boxed{t= \lceil 2233.5\rceil = 2234 } .
\]

Indeed, the construction above gives a sequence with
\(\max|a_i| = 2233.5\) and \(\max|a_i+a_{i+1}| = 1\), showing that
\(t=2233\) would fail, while the bound proved in (2) guarantees that
\(t=2234\) works for all choices of the \(a_i\).

