        [Turn 11 REASONING] (2356 chars)
        Let  

\[
M_1=\max_{1\le i\le n}|a_i| ,\qquad   
M_2=\max_{1\le i\le n}|a_i+a_{i+1}|
\]

(where \(a_{n+1}=a_1\) and \(n=4467\)).  
We need the smallest integer \(t\) such that  

\[
M_1\le t\,M_2\qquad\text{for all }(a_1,\dots ,a_n)\in\mathbb R^{n}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A lower bound for \(M_2\)

Assume \(M_1=1\) (otherwise scale the whole vector).  
Choose an index \(k\) with \(|a_k|=1\) and rotate the indices so that \(k=1\); then \(|a_1|=1\).

Consider the alternating sum of the adjacent sums:

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i+1}(a_i+a_{i+1})
   =\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i+1}a_i+\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i+1}a_{i+1}.
\]

In the second sum put \(j=i+1\) (with \(a_{n+1}=a_1\)).  
Because \(n\) is odd, \((-1)^{n+1}=1\); the two sums cancel for every
\(i=2,\dots ,n\) and we obtain  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i+1}(a_i+a_{i+1}) = 2a_1 .
\]

Hence  

\[
2|a_1| =\Bigl|\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i+1}(a_i+a_{i+1})\Bigr|
        \le \sum_{i=1}^{n}|a_i+a_{i+1}|
        \le n\,M_2 .
\]

Since \(|a_1|=M_1=1\),

\[
\boxed{M_2\ge \frac{2}{n}} .
\]

Thus for every vector  

\[
M_1\le \frac{n}{2}\,M_2 .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Sharpness of the bound

Define  

\[
a_i = (-1)^{\,i+1}\Bigl(1-\frac{2(i-1)}{n}\Bigr),\qquad i=1,\dots ,n .
\]

Because \(n\) is odd, the numbers satisfy \(|a_i|\le 1\) and \(|a_1|=1\).
For \(i=1,\dots ,n-1\),

\[
a_i+a_{i+1}=(-1)^{i+1}\Bigl(1-\frac{2(i-1)}{n}\Bigr)
           -(-1)^{i+1}\Bigl(1-\frac{2i}{n}\Bigr)
           =(-1)^{i+1}\frac{2}{n},
\]

and for the cyclic pair \(i=n\),

\[
a_n+a_1 =\Bigl(-1+\frac{2}{n}\Bigr)+1 = \frac{2}{n}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\max_{i}|a_i+a_{i+1}| = \frac{2}{n},
\qquad
\max_i|a_i| = 1 .
\]

Consequently the inequality (1) cannot be improved:
\[
\frac{M_1}{M_2}= \frac{1}{2/n}= \frac{n}{2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Determination of \(t\)

From (1) we have \(M_1\le \dfrac{n}{2}M_2\) for every real vector,
so any real number \(t\ge \dfrac{n}{2}\) works.
The smallest **integer** satisfying this is  

\[
t = \Bigl\lceil \frac{n}{2}\Bigr\rceil .
\]

For the given \(n=4467\),

\[
\frac{n}{2}=2233.5,\qquad 
t = \lceil 2233.5\rceil = 2234 .
\]

