        \[
F(x_1,\dots ,x_{2025})=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}\sum_{j=1}^{2025}\frac{2i-1}{2j-1}x_i x_j
   =\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{2025}(2i-1)x_i\Bigr)
      \Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^{2025}\frac{x_j}{2j-1}\Bigr)
\]

Denote  

\[
A=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}(2i-1)x_i ,\qquad   
B=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}\frac{x_i}{2i-1},
\]

so that \(F=A\cdot B\).  
The variables satisfy \(x_i\ge 0,\;\sum x_i=1\); thus \((x_i)\) is a probability
distribution on the odd numbers  

\[
y_i=2i-1\;(i=1,\dots ,2025),\qquad
1,3,5,\dots ,4049 .
\]

Hence \(A=E[Y]\) and \(B=E[1/Y]\) for a random variable \(Y\) taking the
values \(y_i\) with probabilities \(x_i\).  
We must maximise \(E[Y]\;E[1/Y]\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Form of an optimal distribution  

Introduce a Lagrange multiplier \(\lambda\) for the condition \(\sum x_i=1\):

\[
L=\Bigl(\sum y_i x_i\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum \frac{x_i}{y_i}\Bigr)-\lambda\Bigl(\sum x_i-1\Bigr).
\]

For any index \(k\) with \(x_k>0\),

\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_k}=y_k B+\frac{A}{y_k}-\lambda=0
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
y_k B+\frac{A}{y_k}= \lambda .
\tag{1}
\]

Thus all \(y_k\) that receive positive weight must satisfy the same value of the
function  

\[
g(y)=yB+\frac{A}{y}.
\]

Since \(g''(y)=\frac{2A}{y^{3}}>0\), \(g\) is **convex**; a convex function can
take a given value at at most two distinct points (unless it is constant).
Consequently an optimal distribution can have **at most two distinct
\(y\)-values** with positive probability.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Two‑point distribution  

Let the two values be \(a<b\) (both odd) and let the probabilities be
\(p\) and \(1-p\). Then

\[
F(p;a,b)=\bigl(p a+(1-p)b\bigr)\bigl(p\frac1a+(1-p)\frac1b\bigr).
\]

Differentiating with respect to \(p\),

\[
\frac{dF}{dp}=0\Longrightarrow p=\frac12 .
\]

Hence for any pair \((a,b)\) the product is maximal when the two masses are
equal. With \(p=\frac12\),

\[
F\Bigl(\frac12;a,b\Bigr)=\frac{(a+b)^2}{4ab}
      =\frac{(1+r)^2}{4r},\qquad r=\frac{b}{a}\ge1 .
\tag{2}
\]

The right‑hand side depends only on the ratio \(r\).  
From \(\displaystyle\frac{d}{dr}\frac{(1+r)^2}{4r}=\frac{r^2-1}{4r^2}>0\) for
\(r>1\), the expression increases with \(r\).  
Thus we must choose the **largest possible ratio** \(b/a\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Choice of the extreme odd numbers  

The smallest odd number is \(1\) and the largest is \(4049\); therefore
\(a=1,\;b=4049\) gives the maximal ratio \(r=4049\). Substituting in (2),

\[
F_{\max}= \frac{(1+4049)^2}{4\cdot4049}
        =\frac{4050^{2}}{4\cdot4049}
        =\frac{2025^{2}}{4049}
        =\frac{4100625}{4049}.
\]

The optimal vector is  

\[
x_1 = x_{2025}= \frac12,\qquad x_i=0\;(2\le i\le2024).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required sum \(m+n\)

The maximal value is \(\displaystyle S=\frac{m}{n}=\frac{4100625}{4049}\) with
\(\gcd(m,n)=1\). Hence

\[
m+n = 4100625+4049 = 4104674 .
\]

\[
\boxed{4104674}
\]

