        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Let }x_1,\dots ,x_{2025}\ge 0,\qquad \sum_{i=1}^{2025}x_i=1,\\[2mm]
&\text{and define }w_i=2i-1\;(>0),\qquad v_i=\frac1{w_i}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The expression to maximise is  

\[
\sum_{i,j=1}^{2025}\frac{2i-1}{2j-1}x_i x_j
   =\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{2025} w_i x_i\Bigr)
    \Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^{2025} v_j x_j\Bigr)
   =A\;B,
\]
where  

\[
A=\sum_{i} w_i x_i,\qquad B=\sum_{i}\frac{x_i}{w_i}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Structure of an optimal point  

Consider the Lagrangian  

\[
L=A\,B-\lambda\Bigl(\sum_i x_i-1\Bigr)-\sum_i\mu_i x_i .
\]

For an interior optimum (\(x_i>0\)) we have \(\mu_i=0\) and  

\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_k}=w_k B+v_k A-\lambda=0
\qquad (k=1,\dots ,2025).
\]

Hence for any two indices \(i,j\) with \(x_i,x_j>0\),

\[
w_i B+\frac{A}{w_i}=w_j B+\frac{A}{w_j}.
\tag{1}
\]

Equation (1) can be rewritten as  

\[
B\,w^2-C\,w+A=0,
\qquad C:=\lambda,
\]

which is a quadratic in \(w\). Therefore it has at most two distinct
solutions. Consequently **at most two different indices can have positive
\(x_i\) in an optimal solution** (the case of a single index gives the
value \(1\)).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reducing to two variables  

Let the two indices be \(i\neq j\) and put  

\[
x_i=t,\qquad x_j=1-t,\qquad 0\le t\le1 .
\]

Then  

\[
A=t w_i+(1-t)w_j,\qquad 
B=t\frac1{w_i}+(1-t)\frac1{w_j},
\]

and  

\[
f(t)=A\,B
      =\bigl[w_j+(w_i-w_j)t\bigr]
       \bigl[\tfrac1{w_j}+\bigl(\tfrac1{w_i}-\tfrac1{w_j}\bigr)t\bigr].
\]

\(f(t)\) is a concave quadratic; its maximum on \([0,1]\) occurs at  

\[
t=\frac12 .
\]

Thus the best distribution for a given pair \((i,j)\) is  

\[
x_i=x_j=\frac12,
\qquad\text{and the maximal value for this pair is}
\qquad
\frac{(w_i+w_j)^2}{4w_iw_j}.
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Choosing the pair \((i,j)\)

From (2) define  

\[
R(i,j)=\frac{(w_i+w_j)^2}{4w_iw_j}
      =\frac12+\frac{w_i}{4w_j}+\frac{w_j}{4w_i},
      \qquad w_i=2i-1.
\]

For fixed \(i\) the function \(R(i,j)\) increases with \(j\) (because
\(w_j>w_i\) makes the term \(\frac{w_j}{4w_i}\) larger and
\(\frac{w_i}{4w_j}\) smaller).  
For fixed \(j\) it decreases with \(i\).  
Hence the maximum is attained at the smallest possible \(i\) and the
largest possible \(j\):

\[
i=1,\qquad j=2025.
\]

Here  

\[
w_1=2\cdot1-1=1,\qquad
w_{2025}=2\cdot2025-1=4049.
\]

