\[
A=\sum a_i x_i,\qquad B=\sum \frac{x_i}{a_i},
\]
so the expression to maximise is \(AB\).

---

### 1.  Characterisation of optimal \(x\)

Using Lagrange multipliers for the problem  

\[
\max_{x\ge0,\;\sum x_i=1} AB,
\]
the stationary condition for any index with \(x_i>0\) is  

\[
a_i B+\frac{A}{a_i}= \lambda .
\]

Hence for any two indices \(i,j\) with \(x_i,x_j>0\),

\[
a_i B+\frac{A}{a_i}=a_j B+\frac{A}{a_j}
\Longrightarrow B=\frac{A}{a_i a_j}.
\]

Thus, if more than two distinct \(a_i\) had positive weight, the
product \(a_i a_j\) would have to be the same for all such pairs,
which is impossible. Consequently **at most two different indices can have
positive \(x_i\)** in an optimal solution.

If only one index is used, \(AB=1\) (since \(a_i\cdot 1/a_i =1\)),
so a larger value must involve exactly two indices.

---

### 2.  Two‑point distribution

Let the two indices have odd values \(a<b\) and probabilities \(\alpha\) and
\(1-\alpha\). Then

\[
A=\alpha a+(1-\alpha)b,\qquad 
B=\alpha\frac1a+(1-\alpha)\frac1b .
\]

The stationary condition gives \(\alpha=\frac12\); the product becomes  

\[
AB=\frac{a+b}{2}\cdot\frac{1/a+1/b}{2}
       =\frac{(a+b)^2}{4ab}
       =\frac{a}{b}+\frac{b}{a}+2\Big/4 .
\]

For fixed \(a,b\) this value is increasing with the ratio \(b/a\).  
Hence the maximum is attained for the extreme pair
\(a=1,\;b=4049\).

