The expression to maximise is  

\[
F=\sum_{i,j}\frac{a_i}{a_j}x_i x_j
   =\Bigl(\sum_i a_i x_i\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_j\frac{x_j}{a_j}\Bigr)
   =A\cdot B .
\]

Let  

\[
A=\sum_i a_i x_i,\qquad B=\sum_i\frac{x_i}{a_i}.
\]

Both \(A\) and \(B\) are linear in the variables \(x_i\); we maximise the product
\(A B\) over the simplex.

---

### 1.  Lagrange multiplier condition  

For an interior optimum (\(x_k>0\)) we have  

\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial x_k}\bigl(AB-\lambda(\sum x_i-1)\bigr)=
a_k B+\frac{A}{a_k}-\lambda =0 .
\]

Hence for any two indices \(i,j\) with \(x_i,x_j>0\),

\[
a_i B+\frac{A}{a_i}=a_j B+\frac{A}{a_j}.
\]

Re‑arranging gives  

\[
B a_i^2-\lambda a_i+A=0 .
\]

Thus all \(a_i\) with positive weight satisfy the same quadratic
equation; a quadratic has at most two distinct roots.
Consequently **an optimal distribution uses at most two different
indices**.

---

### 2.  Two‑point distribution  

Take two indices \(i<j\) with \(a= a_i,\;b=a_j\) and let
\(x_i=p,\;x_j=1-p\) (\(0\le p\le1\)).  
Then  

\[
A=pa+(1-p)b,\qquad 
B=p\frac1a+(1-p)\frac1b .
\]

Writing \(u=(b-a)p\;(0\le u\le b-a)\),

\[
A=b-u,\qquad B=\frac{1}{b}\Bigl(1+\frac{u}{a}\Bigr),
\]
\[
F=A B=\frac{(b-u)(a+u)}{ab}
      =1+\frac{(b-a)^2}{4ab}
\quad\text{(maximum attained at }u=\tfrac{b-a}{2},\;p=\tfrac12\text{)} .
\]

Hence for any pair \((a,b)\),

\[
F_{\max}(a,b)=1+\frac{(b-a)^2}{4ab}
            =\frac{a}{4b}+\frac{b}{4a}+\frac12
            =\frac{r+\frac1r}{4}+\frac12,
\]
where \(r=b/a>1\).

The function \(\displaystyle\frac{r+\frac1r}{4}+\frac12\) is increasing for
\(r>1\); therefore the overall maximum occurs for the largest possible
ratio \(r\).

---

### 3.  Choosing the extreme indices  

The largest ratio is obtained with the smallest and largest odd numbers:

\[
a_1=2\cdot1-1=1,\qquad a_{2025}=2\cdot2025-1=4049,
\]
\[
r_{\max}= \frac{4049}{1}=4049 .
\]

Thus  

\[
S_{\max}= \frac{4049+\frac1{4049}}{4}+\frac12
        =\frac{4049^2+1}{4\cdot4049}+\frac12
        =\frac{4100625}{4049}.
\]

The optimum is achieved by taking  

\[
x_1=x_{2025}=\frac12,\qquad x_k=0\;(k\neq1,2025),
\]
which indeed gives  

\[
\Bigl(\frac{1+4049}{2}\Bigr)\Bigl(\frac12\Bigl(1+\frac1{4049}\Bigr)\Bigr)
   =\frac{4100625}{4049}.
\]

The fraction is already in lowest terms (\(\gcd(4100625,4049)=1\)).
Hence  

\[
m=4100625,\qquad n=4049,\qquad m+n=4104674 .
\]

