        \[
\text{Let }a_i=2i-1\;(i=1,\dots ,2025),\qquad 
x_i\ge 0,\ \sum_{i=1}^{2025}x_i=1 .
\]

The expression to maximise is  

\[
\sum_{i,j}\frac{a_i}{a_j}x_i x_j
   =\Bigl(\sum_{i}a_i x_i\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_{j}\frac{x_j}{a_j}\Bigr)
   =A\cdot B ,
\]
where  

\[
A=\sum_{i}a_i x_i,\qquad 
B=\sum_{i}\frac{x_i}{a_i}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  KKT condition

For an interior optimum (all \(x_i>0\)) the Lagrange multiplier \(\lambda\) for the
constraint \(\sum x_i=1\) gives  

\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}(AB)=a_iB+\frac{A}{a_i}= \lambda
\qquad (i=1,\dots ,2025).
\]

Hence for any \(i,j\) with \(x_i,x_j>0\)

\[
a_iB+\frac{A}{a_i}=a_jB+\frac{A}{a_j}.
\]

Rearranging,

\[
(a_i-a_j)B = A\Bigl(\frac1{a_j}-\frac1{a_i}\Bigr)
          =A\frac{a_i-a_j}{a_i a_j},
\]

and, because \(a_i\neq a_j\),

\[
AB = A = B\,a_i a_j .
\tag{1}
\]

Equation (1) can hold for more than two indices only when all the
products \(a_i a_j\) are equal, which is impossible.
Therefore **at most two variables can be positive at a maximum**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reducing to two indices

Assume the positive variables are \(x_p\) and \(x_q\;(p<q)\) with
\(x_p=t,\;x_q=1-t\).  Put \(a=a_p,\;b=a_q\).  Then

\[
F(t)=\bigl(at+b(1-t)\bigr)\Bigl(\frac{t}{a}+\frac{1-t}{b}\Bigr).
\]

\[
\frac{dF}{dt}= \frac{(a-b)^2}{ab}(1-2t)=0\Longrightarrow t=\frac12 .
\]

Thus the optimal split for any pair \((p,q)\) is \(x_p=x_q=\tfrac12\).

With \(t=\tfrac12\),

\[
F_{\max}(a,b)=\frac{(a+b)^2}{4ab}
            =\frac{a}{b}+\frac{b}{a}+2\over 4 .
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Choosing the best pair

Here \(a_i=2i-1\) is strictly increasing, so for \(p<q\) we have
\(a_p<a_q\).  In (2) the quantity \(\dfrac{a}{b}+\dfrac{b}{a}\) equals
\(r+1/r\) with \(r=a/b\le1\); this expression is **largest when
\(r\) is smallest**, i.e. when \(a\) is minimal and \(b\) is maximal.
Consequently the optimal pair is \((1,2025)\).

For \(a_1=1,\ a_{2025}=2\cdot2025-1=4049\),

\[
S_{\max}=F_{\max}(1,4049)=\frac{(1+4049)^2}{4\cdot1\cdot4049}
      =\frac{(4050)^2}{4\cdot4049}
      =\frac{2025^{2}}{2\cdot2025-1}
      =\frac{4100625}{4049}.
\]

The numerator and denominator are coprime (\(\gcd(4100625,4049)=1\)).
Hence \(m=4100625,\;n=4049\).

