--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  KKT‑condition

Maximise \(f(x)=\log A+\log B\) (the logarithm is monotone, so the maximiser of
\(AB\) also maximises \(f\)).  
With a Lagrange multiplier \(\lambda\),

\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial x_k}\Bigl(\log A+\log B+\lambda\!\bigl(\sum_i x_i-1\bigr)\Bigr)
   =\frac{a_k}{A}+\frac{c_k}{B}+\lambda =0 .
\]

Hence for every index with \(x_k>0\),

\[
\frac{a_k}{A}+\frac{1}{a_k B}=t\qquad(\text{constant }t).
\tag{1}
\]

Multiplying (1) by \(a_k\) gives a quadratic equation in \(a_k\),

\[
\frac{a_k^{2}}{A}-t a_k+\frac1B=0 .
\]

A quadratic has at most two distinct roots, therefore **at most two
different numbers \(a_i\) can have positive weight** in an optimal solution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Form of an optimal solution

Assume the optimal solution uses two distinct indices \(i\neq j\) with
\(x_i=x_j=\frac12\) (the sum of the \(x\)’s must be \(1\)).  
Let \(a= a_i,\;b=a_j\;(a<b)\).  
Then

\[
A=\frac{a+b}{2},\qquad 
B=\frac{1}{2}\Bigl(\frac1a+\frac1b\Bigr),
\]

and the objective value is

\[
AB=\frac{(a+b)^2}{4ab}
   =\frac{(r+1)^2}{4r},\qquad r=\frac{b}{a}\ge1 .
\tag{2}
\]

The function \(\displaystyle f(r)=\frac{(r+1)^2}{4r}
      =\frac{r+2+1/r}{4}\) is increasing for \(r>1\)
(because \(f'(r)=\frac{r^2-1}{4r^2}>0\)).  
Hence the maximum is attained when the ratio \(r\) is as large as possible,
i.e. when we take the smallest and the largest odd numbers:

\[
a_{\min}=2\cdot1-1=1,\qquad 
