        \[
\begin{aligned}
E &=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}\sum_{j=1}^{2025}\frac{2i-1}{2j-1}\,x_i x_j
      =\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{2025}(2i-1)x_i\Bigr)
        \Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^{2025}\frac{x_j}{2j-1}\Bigr)\\[2mm]
    &=A\cdot C ,
\end{aligned}
\]
where  

\[
A=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}a_i x_i,\qquad a_i=2i-1,
\qquad 
C=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}c_i x_i,\qquad c_i=\frac1{2i-1}.
\]

Thus \((A,C)\) is a convex combination of the points  

\[
P_i=(a_i,c_i)=\Bigl(2i-1,\frac1{2i-1}\Bigr),\qquad i=1,\dots ,2025 .
\]

Hence the set of attainable \((A,C)\) is the convex hull of these points.
For any two indices \(i<j\) let us take only \(x_i\) and \(x_j\) non‑zero.
Writing \(x_i=x_j=\tfrac12\) (the only stationary point on the segment
\(P_iP_j\)) gives  

\[
E_{ij}= \frac{a_i+a_j}{2}\,\frac{c_i+c_j}{2}
      =\frac{(a_i+a_j)^2}{4a_i a_j}
      =\frac{(1+r)^2}{4r},
\qquad r=\frac{a_j}{a_i}>1 .
\]

The derivative of \(\displaystyle f(r)=\frac{(1+r)^2}{4r}\) is  

\[
f'(r)=\frac{r^2-1}{4r^2}>0\quad (r>1),
\]

so \(f(r)\) increases with the ratio \(r\).
The largest possible ratio is obtained for the smallest and the largest
indices:

\[
r_{\max}= \frac{2\cdot2025-1}{2\cdot1-1}= \frac{4049}{1}=4049 .
\]

Hence the maximal value of the original sum is  

\[
S_{\max}=f(4049)=\frac{(1+4049)^2}{4\cdot4049}
          =\frac{4050^{2}}{4\cdot4049}
          =\frac{4100625}{4049}.
\]

The fraction \(\dfrac{4100625}{4049}\) is already reduced
(\(\gcd(4100625,4049)=1\)).
Therefore, with \(m=4100625,\; n=4049\),

\[
m+n = 4100625+4049 = \boxed{4104674}.
\]

