        [Turn 20 REASONING] (2607 chars)
        \[
S=\sum_{i,j=1}^{2025}\frac{2i-1}{2j-1}\,x_i x_j
      =\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{2025}(2i-1)x_i\Bigr)
        \Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^{2025}\frac{x_j}{2j-1}\Bigr)
\]

Denote  

\[
A=\sum_{i}a_i x_i,\qquad B=\sum_{i}\frac{x_i}{a_i},
\qquad a_i=2i-1 .
\]

Thus \(S=A\cdot B\) with \(x_i\ge 0,\ \sum x_i=1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The optimal distribution uses at most two indices  

Consider the Lagrange function  

\[
L=(A B)-\lambda\Bigl(\sum_i x_i-1\Bigr)-\sum_i\mu_i x_i .
\]

For an interior optimum (\(x_i>0\)) we have  

\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_k}=a_k B+\frac{A}{a_k}-\lambda =0
\qquad(k=1,\dots ,2025).
\]

Hence for any two indices \(i,j\) with \(x_i,x_j>0\),

\[
a_i B+\frac{A}{a_i}=a_j B+\frac{A}{a_j}.
\]

Rearranging gives  

\[
(a_i-a_j)B=A\Bigl(\frac1{a_j}-\frac1{a_i}\Bigr)
      =A\frac{a_i-a_j}{a_i a_j},
\]

so, because \(a_i\neq a_j\),

\[
B=\frac{A}{a_i a_j}.
\]

Thus the product \(a_i a_j\) must be the same for every pair of
indices that receive positive mass.  This is possible only when at most
two distinct \(a_i\)’s appear.  Consequently an optimal vector \(x\) has
support of size at most two.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Two–point case  

Let the positive components be at indices \(p<q\) with
\(x_p=t,\;x_q=1-t\).  Then

\[
\begin{aligned}
S&= (t a_p+(1-t)a_q)\Bigl(t\frac1{a_p}+(1-t)\frac1{a_q}\Bigr)\\
  &=1+t(1-t)\frac{(a_q-a_p)^2}{a_p a_q}.
\end{aligned}
\]

For fixed \(p,q\) the factor \(t(1-t)\) is maximised at \(t=\frac12\),
giving  

\[
S_{\max}(p,q)=1+\frac{(a_q-a_p)^2}{4a_p a_q}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Choosing the pair \((p,q)\)

Since \(a_i=2i-1\),

\[
\frac{(a_q-a_p)^2}{a_p a_q}
   =\frac{4(q-p)^2}{(2p-1)(2q-1)} .
\]

For a fixed distance \(d=q-p\) the denominator grows with \(p\); hence
the expression is largest when \(p=1\).
With \(p=1\) it becomes  

\[
\frac{4d^{2}}{1\cdot(2d+1)},
\]

which is increasing in \(d\).  Therefore the maximum occurs for the
largest possible distance, i.e. \(p=1,\;q=2025\).

Thus  

\[
S_{\max}=1+\frac{(a_{2025}-a_{1})^{2}}{4a_{1}a_{2025}}
       =1+\frac{(4049-1)^{2}}{4\cdot1\cdot4049}
       =1+\frac{(2\cdot2024)^{2}}{4\cdot4049}
       =\frac{2025^{2}}{2\cdot2025-1}
       =\frac{4100625}{4049}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required sum  

The fraction is already in lowest terms (\(\gcd(4100625,4049)=1\)).
Hence  

\[
m=4100625,\qquad n=4049,\qquad m+n=4100625+4049=4104674.
\]

\[
\boxed{4104674}
\]

