        \[
P(z)=z^{4051}+z^{4050}+5\qquad (\deg P=4051)
\]

Because the coefficients are real, non‑real zeros occur in conjugate pairs, so the number of
non‑real zeros equals  

\[
\deg P-\text{(number of real zeros)} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Real zeros of \(P\)

For real \(x\),

\[
f(x)=x^{4051}+x^{4050}+5=x^{4050}(x+1)+5 .
\]

* As \(x\to+\infty\), \(f(x)\to+\infty\).
* As \(x\to-\infty\), \(x^{4050}>0\) and \(x+1<0\), hence  
  \(f(x)\sim x^{4050}(x+1)\to-\infty\).

Thus \(f\) has at least one real root.

The derivative

\[
f'(x)=4051x^{4050}+4050x^{4049}=x^{4049}(4051x+4050)
\]

vanishes only at  

\[
x=0\quad\text{and}\quad x=-\frac{4050}{4051}\approx-0.99975 .
\]

Hence \(f\) is

* increasing on \((-\infty,-\frac{4050}{4051})\),
* decreasing on \(\bigl(-\frac{4050}{4051},0\bigr)\),
* increasing on \((0,\infty)\).

The values at the critical points are

\[
f\!\left(-\frac{4050}{4051}\right)=\Bigl(\frac{4050}{4051}\Bigr)^{4050}\!\!\frac1{4051}+5>0,
\qquad
f(0)=5>0 .
\]

Thus the local maximum and the local minimum are both positive.
Since \(f\) is negative for large negative \(x\) and positive for large positive \(x\),
the graph must cross the \(x\)-axis exactly once, before the local maximum.
Consequently **there is exactly one real root**, and it is simple
(\(f'(x)\neq0\) at that point).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Non‑real zeros

Total degree \(=4051\).  
Real zeros \(=1\).  

\[
\boxed{4051-1 = 4050}
\]

Hence there are **4050 non‑real complex numbers** satisfying  

\[
z^{4051}+z^{4050}+5=0,
\]

counting multiplicities.

