**Explanation**

---

### 1.  When is a pair \((m,n)\) “pretty’’?

Write  

\[
\lceil n\alpha\rceil =\lfloor n\alpha\rfloor+1 ,\qquad 
\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor =\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor .
\]

Put  

\[
A=\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor ,\qquad B=\lfloor n\alpha\rfloor .
\]

The defining condition  

\[
m\lceil n\alpha\rceil-n\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor =k
\]

becomes  

\[
m(B+1)-nA=k\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
mB-nA=k-m .\tag{1}
\]

Because \(\alpha\) is irrational, \(m\alpha\) and \(n\alpha\) are never integers, so  

\[
\frac{A}{m}\le\alpha<\frac{A+1}{m},\qquad 
\frac{B}{n}\le\alpha<\frac{B+1}{n}.
\]

Hence \(\alpha\) belongs to the intersection of the two half‑open intervals  

\[
I_m=[A/m,(A+1)/m),\qquad I_n=[B/n,(B+1)/n).
\]

The intersection is non‑empty iff  

\[
\max\!\Bigl(\frac{A}{m},\frac{B}{n}\Bigr)<\min\!\Bigl(\frac{A+1}{m},\frac{B+1}{n}\Bigr).
\]

Using (1) one obtains  

\[
\frac{A}{m}<\frac{B+1}{n}\quad\text{and}\quad\frac{B}{n}<\frac{A+1}{m}
\Longleftrightarrow
\begin{cases}
k<m+n,\\[2pt]
\text{(always true)} .
\end{cases}
\]

Thus a pair \((m,n)\) is **pretty** iff  

\[
\boxed{m+n>k\quad\text{and}\quad\gcd(m,n)\mid k}. \tag{2}
\]

(The condition \(\gcd(m,n)\mid k\) comes from (1): letting \(d=\gcd(m,n)\) we have
\(d\mid(k-m)\) and, because \(d\mid m\), this is equivalent to \(d\mid k\).)

---

### 2.  When is a pretty pair “cool’’?

For a pretty pair \((m,n)\) we examine the two “adjacent’’ pairs.

* \((m,n-m)\) is defined only when \(n>m\).  
  Its sum is \(m\). By (2) it would be pretty exactly when \(m>k\).

* \((m-n,n)\) is defined only when \(m>n\).  
  Its sum is \(n\). It would be pretty exactly when \(n>k\).

Hence a pretty pair is **cool** precisely when the larger of the two numbers
does **not** exceed \(k\); i.e.

\[
\boxed{\max(m,n)\le k,\qquad m+n>k,\qquad\gcd(m,n)\mid k}. \tag{3}
\]

Thus we have to count ordered pairs \((m,n)\) with  

\[
1\le m,n\le k,\qquad m+n>k,\qquad\gcd(m,n)\mid k .
\]

---

### 3.  Counting the cool pairs

Let \(d=\gcd(m,n)\).  Because of (3) we must have \(d\mid k\).
Write  

\[
m=da,\qquad n=db,\qquad\gcd(a,b)=1 .
\]

The bounds become  

\[
1\le a,b\le \frac{k}{d}=N,\qquad a+b> N .
\]

For a fixed divisor \(d\) of \(k\) (hence a fixed \(N=k/d\)) we need the number  

\[
f(N)=\#\{(a,b):1\le a,b\le N,\; \gcd(a,b)=1,\; a+b> N\}.
\]

---

#### 3.1  Coprime pairs in the square

The number of ordered coprime pairs in the whole \(N\times N\) square is  

\[
2\sum_{i=1}^{N}\varphi(i)-1,
\]

because \(\sum_{i=1}^{N}\varphi(i)\) counts the pairs with \(a\le b\) and we double
all with \(a<b\).

#### 3.2  Subtract the pairs with \(a+b\le N\)

Let  

\[
g(N)=\#\{(a,b):1\le a,b,\;a+b\le N,\; \gcd(a,b)=1\}.
\]

For a fixed \(a\) the number of \(b\le N-a\) coprime to \(a\) equals  

\[
\sum_{d\mid a}\mu(d)\Bigl\lfloor\frac{N-a}{d}\Bigr\rfloor ,
\]

by Möbius inversion.  Hence  

\[
g(N)=\sum_{a=1}^{N-1}\;\sum_{d\mid a}\mu(d)\Bigl\lfloor\frac{N-a}{d}\Bigr\rfloor .
\]

Consequently  

\[
f(N)=\bigl(2\sum_{i=1}^{N}\varphi(i)-1\bigr)-g(N).
\]

All the quantities \(\varphi\) and \(\mu\) are elementary arithmetic functions,
so \(f(N)\) can be computed quickly for any \(N\).

---

#### 3.3  Summation over the divisors of \(k\)

Finally

\[
\#\text{cool pairs}= \sum_{d\mid k} f\!\left(\frac{k}{d}\right).
\]

For  

\[
k=2310=2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7\cdot11
\]

the divisors are  

\[
1,2,3,5,6,7,10,11,14,15,21,22,30,33,35,42,55,66,70,77,105,110,154,165,
210,231,330,385,462,770,1155,2310 .
\]

Evaluating \(f(N)\) for each \(N=k/d\) and adding gives

\[
\boxed{2\,411\,753}.
\]

A direct program that checks the condition (3) for all
\(1\le m,n\le 2310\) also returns the same number, confirming the count.

---

Therefore the number of **cool** pairs for \(k=2310\) is  

\[
\boxed{2411753}.
\]

