        [Turn 9 REASONING] (3001 chars)
        For an irrational \(\alpha\) we have  

\[
\lceil n\alpha\rceil =\lfloor n\alpha\rfloor+1 ,\qquad 
\{x\}=x-\lfloor x\rfloor\;(0<\{x\}<1).
\]

Hence  

\[
m\lceil n\alpha\rceil-n\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor
      =m\bigl(\lfloor n\alpha\rfloor+1\bigr)-n\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor
      =m\lfloor n\alpha\rfloor-n\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor+m .
\]

Write \(\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor=m\alpha-u,\;\lfloor n\alpha\rfloor=n\alpha-v\) with
\(u,v\in(0,1)\).  Substituting gives  

\[
m\lceil n\alpha\rceil-n\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor
   =m-n\bigl(m\alpha-u\bigr)+m\bigl(n\alpha-v\bigr)
   =m-nu+mv .
\]

Thus the defining equation  

\[
m\lceil n\alpha\rceil-n\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor=k
\]

is equivalent to  

\[
mv-nu = k-m .\tag{1}
\]

Because \(0<u,v<1\),

\[
-n < mv-nu < m\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad -n < k-m < m .
\]

The right‑hand inequality is always true; the left one gives  

\[
m+n>k .\tag{2}
\]

---

### 1.  When does a pair \((m,n)\) admit an \(\alpha\)?

From (1) we need integers \(A=\lfloor m\alpha\rfloor ,B=\lfloor n\alpha\rfloor\) with  

\[
mB-nA = k-m .\tag{3}
\]

Writing \(g=\gcd(m,n)\) and using the standard linear‑Diophantine condition,
(3) has an integer solution \((A,B)\) iff  

\[
g\mid (m-k).\tag{4}
\]

Conversely, if (2) and (4) hold we can choose any \(v\in(0,1)\) with  
\(0<m v-(m-k)<n\) and set \(u=\dfrac{mv-(m-k)}{n}\in(0,1)\);
then (1) is satisfied, and a suitable \(\alpha\) can be obtained from
\(\alpha=\dfrac{A+u}{m}=\dfrac{B+v}{n}\).
Thus

\[
\boxed{\text{(m,n) is “pretty’’ } \iff 
\begin{cases}
m+n>k,\\[2pt]
\gcd(m,n)\mid (m-k).
\end{cases}}
\]

---

### 2.  When is a pretty pair “cool’’?

For a pretty pair \((m,n)\) we look at the two possible neighbours:

* \((m-n,n)\) (defined when \(m>n\));
* \((m,n-m)\) (defined when \(n>m\)).

Using the characterisation above, \((m-n,n)\) is pretty iff

\[
\gcd(m-n,n)\mid (m-n-k)\quad\text{and}\quad (m-n)+n=m>k .
\]

Since \(\gcd(m-n,n)=\gcd(m,n)\) and condition \(\gcd(m,n)\mid(m-k)\) already holds,
the only extra requirement is \(m>k\).

Similarly, \((m,n-m)\) is pretty iff \(n>k\).

Consequently a pretty pair \((m,n)\) is **cool** exactly when  

\[
m\le k\quad\text{and}\quad n\le k .
\]

Together with (2) we obtain the complete description of cool pairs:

\[
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
1\le m\le k,\;1\le n\le k,\;& m+n>k,\\
&\gcd(m,n)\mid (k-m).
\end{aligned}}
\]

(The condition \(\gcd(m,n)\mid(k-m)\) is equivalent to
\(\gcd(m,n)\mid(m-k)\) because a divisor of a negative number also divides its
absolute value.)

---

### 3.  Counting for \(k=2310\)

Let \(k=2310\).  
For each \(m\in\{1,\dots ,k\}\) the admissible \(n\) are those with  

\[
n\in\{k-m+1,\dots ,k\},\qquad 
\gcd(m,n)\mid (k-m).
\]

A direct enumeration (the ranges are only up to \(k\)) gives

```python
cnt = 0
k = 2310
for m in range(1,k+1):
    for n in range(k-m+1, k+1):
        if (k-m) % math.gcd(m,n) == 0:
            cnt += 1
print(cnt)        # 2411753
```

