        \[
\begin{aligned}
a_1&=n^{2}-10n+23=(n-5)^2-2,\\
a_2&=n^{2}-9n+97,\\
a_3&=n^{2}-12n+46=(n-6)^2+10 .
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 1.  Parity considerations  

*If \(n\) is odd:*  
\(n-5\) is even, so \((n-5)^2\) is even and  

\[
a_1=(n-5)^2-2\equiv 0-2\equiv 0\pmod 2 .
\]

Thus \(a_1\) is even.  
The only even prime is \(2\); therefore we must have  

\[
a_1=2\Longrightarrow (n-5)^2=4\Longrightarrow n=3\text{ or }n=7 .
\]

*If \(n\) is even:*  
\(n-5\) is odd, so \((n-5)^2\) is odd and \(a_1\) is odd – possible.  
But for even \(n\),

\[
a_3=n^{2}-12n+46\equiv 0\pmod 2 ,
\]

so \(a_3\) is even and \(\ge 10\); it cannot be prime.  
Hence **no even \(n\) works**.

Consequently the only candidates are the odd values \(n=3,7\).

---

### 2.  Verification of the candidates  

| \(n\) | \(a_1\) | \(a_2\) | \(a_3\) | prime? |
|------|--------|--------|--------|--------|
| 3 | \(3^{2}-30+23=2\) | \(9-27+97=79\) | \(9-36+46=19\) | all prime |
| 7 | \(49-70+23=2\) | \(49-63+97=83\) | \(49-84+46=11\) | all prime |

Both satisfy the requirement.

---

### 3.  Sums \(a_1+a_2+a_3\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
n=3 &: \; 2+79+19 = 100,\\
n=7 &: \; 2+83+11 = 96.
\end{aligned}
\]

The distinct possible values are \(100\) and \(96\).

\[
\boxed{100+96 = 196}
\]

Thus the sum of all possible values of \(a_1+a_2+a_3\) is **196**.

