---

### 1.  Parity considerations  

*If \(n\) is even* then \(n-6\) is even, so \((n-6)^{2}\) is even and  
\(a_{3}=(n-6)^{2}+10\) is even.  
A prime that is even must be \(2\); however \((n-6)^{2}+10=2\) has no integer
solution. Hence **\(n\) cannot be even**.

Thus \(n\) is odd. Then \(n-5\) is even, so \((n-5)^{2}\) is even and  

\[
a_{1}=(n-5)^{2}-2\quad\text{is even}.
\]

Again the only even prime is \(2\); therefore we must have  

\[
a_{1}=2\Longrightarrow (n-5)^{2}=4\Longrightarrow n-5=\pm2 .
\]

Hence  

\[
n=3\quad\text{or}\quad n=7 .
\]

Both are odd, so the parity condition is satisfied.

---

### 2.  Verification of the two candidates  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
n & a_{1}=n^{2}-10n+23 & a_{2}=n^{2}-9n+97 & a_{3}=n^{2}-12n+46\\\hline
3 & 2 & 79 & 19\\
7 & 2 & 83 & 11
\end{array}
\]

All six numbers are prime, so the only admissible values of \(n\) are
\(3\) and \(7\).

---

### 3.  Sum of the required quantities  

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_{3}&=a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}=2+79+19=100,\\
S_{7}&=a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}=2+83+11=96.
\end{aligned}
\]

The problem asks for the sum of all possible values of \(a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}\):

\[
\boxed{100+96=196}.
\]

