        \[
a_{1}<a_{2}<\dots <a_{38},\qquad 
\sum_{i=1}^{38}a_i =1997 .
\]

For \(k=1,\dots ,37\) put  

\[
b_k=\min \Bigl\{\sum_{i=1}^{k}a_i,\; \sum_{i=k+1}^{38}a_i\Bigr\},
\qquad 
S=\max_{1\le k\le 37}b_k .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reformulation

Let  

\[
P_k=\sum_{i=1}^{k}a_i ,\qquad T=1997 .
\]

Then  

\[
b_k=\min (P_k,T-P_k),\qquad 
S=\max_{k} b_k .
\]

Write  

\[
d_k =\bigl|2P_k-T\bigr| .
\]

Because \(T\) is odd, each \(d_k\) is an odd positive integer and  

\[
b_k=\frac{T-d_k}{2}.
\]

Hence  

\[
S=\frac{T-\displaystyle\min_{k} d_k}{2}= \frac{1997-D}{2},
\tag{1}
\]

where  

\[
D:=\min_{k} d_k .
\]

To **minimise** \(S\) we must **maximise** the minimal distance  

\[
D=\min_{k}\bigl|2P_k-1997\bigr|.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A universal upper bound for \(D\)

Let  

\[
L=\frac{1997-D}{2},\qquad H=\frac{1997+D}{2}\;(=L+D).
\]

For every \(k\) we must have  

\[
P_k\le L\quad\text{or}\quad P_k\ge H .
\tag{2}
\]

Let \(m\) be the smallest index for which \(P_m>L\); then
\(P_{m-1}\le L\).
Since the numbers are distinct and increasing,  

\[
a_i\ge i\qquad (i=1,\dots ,38)
\]

so  

\[
P_{m-1}\ge 1+2+\dots +(m-1)=\frac{(m-1)m}{2}.      \tag{3}
\]

Consequently  

\[
\frac{(m-1)m}{2}\le L .                                 \tag{4}
\]

From (2) we also need \(P_m\ge H\); therefore  

\[
a_m = P_m-P_{m-1}\ge H-P_{m-1}\ge H-L=D.                \tag{5}
\]

The remaining \(r:=38-m\) numbers are larger than \(a_m\); the smallest
they can be is  

\[
a_m+1,\;a_m+2,\dots ,a_m+r .
\]

Hence the total sum satisfies  

\[
1997\ge\frac{(m-1)m}{2}+a_m+(a_m+1+\dots +a_m+r)
      =\frac{(m-1)m}{2}+a_m(1+r)+\frac{r(r+1)}{2}.      \tag{6}
\]

Using (5) (\(a_m\ge D\)) in (6) gives

\[
1997\ge\frac{(m-1)m}{2}+D(1+r)+\frac{r(r+1)}{2}.
\]

Thus

\[
D\le\frac{1997-\dfrac{(m-1)m}{2}-\dfrac{r(r+1)}{2}}{1+r}
   =\frac{1997-\dfrac{(m-1)m}{2}-\dfrac{(38-m)(39-m)}{2}}{39-m}.
\tag{7}
\]

The right‑hand side of (7) is an increasing function of \(m\);
its largest value occurs at \(m=37\) (the only value for which
\(1+r=2\)).  Substituting \(m=37\) gives

\[
D\le\frac{1997-\frac{36\cdot37}{2}-\frac{1\cdot2}{2}}{2}
   =\frac{1997-666-1}{2}=665 .
\]

Therefore for **any** admissible sequence

\[
D\le 665\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
S\ge\frac{1997-665}{2}=666 .                         \tag{8}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  A construction attaining the bound

Take  

\[
a_i=i\;(1\le i\le 36),\qquad a_{37}=665,\qquad a_{38}=666 .
\]

These are distinct, increasing and

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{38}a_i=
\underbrace{1+2+\dots +36}_{=666}+665+666=1997 .
\]

The prefix sums are  

\[
P_{k}=1+2+\dots +k\;(k\le 36),\;P_{36}=666,\;
P_{37}=1331 .
\]

Hence  

\[
b_k=P_k\;(k\le 36),\qquad b_{36}=b_{37}=666,
\]
so  

\[
S=\max b_k =666 .
\]

Thus the bound (8) is sharp.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimal possible value of \(S\)

From (8) we have \(S\ge 666\), and the explicit sequence above shows
\(S=666\) is attainable. Consequently

\[
\boxed{\,666\,}
\]

is the minimal possible value of \(S\).

