**Explanation**

For each \(n\ge 0\) set  

\[
T_n=(a_{n+1},a_{n+2},\dots ,a_{n+2025})\in S .
\]

If \(S\) were finite, the directed graph whose vertices are the elements of \(S\) and whose
edges are defined by  

\[
T_n\longrightarrow T_{n+1}=(a_{n+2},\dots ,a_{n+2026})
\]

would have only finitely many vertices.  
Because each vertex has exactly one outgoing edge (the next block is uniquely
determined by the current block), the infinite walk  

\[
T_0,T_1,T_2,\dots
\]

must eventually repeat a vertex: there exist \(i<j\) with \(T_i=T_j\).

Now \(T_i=T_j\) means  

\[
(a_{i+1},\dots ,a_{i+2025})=(a_{j+1},\dots ,a_{j+2025}).
\]

Consequently the next entries are equal:
\(a_{i+2026}=a_{j+2026}\); otherwise the two successors \(T_{i+1}\) and \(T_{j+1}\)
would be different, contradicting the determinism of the walk.
Repeating this argument inductively yields  

\[
a_{i+k}=a_{j+k}\qquad\text{for all }k\ge 1 .
\]

Hence the sequence \((a_n)\) is periodic from index \(i+1\) onward with period
\(d=j-i>0\); i.e. it is **eventually periodic**, contrary to the hypothesis.

Thus \(S\) cannot be finite.  Since the sequence is infinite, the set of its
2025‑tuples is countably infinite.  Therefore the smallest possible number of
elements of \(S\) is infinite.

