        Let  

\[
a_1,a_2,a_3,\dots ,a_7\qquad\text{and}\qquad b_1,b_2,b_3,\dots ,b_7
\]

be the two non‑decreasing sequences of non‑negative integers.
For each sequence  

\[
a_n=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}\qquad (n\ge 3)
\]

and similarly for the \(b\)’s.  
Because the sequences are non‑decreasing we must have  

\[
a_1\le a_2,\qquad b_1\le b_2 .
\]

---

### 1.  The seventh term in terms of the first two

From the recurrence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_3 &= a_1+a_2,\\
a_4 &= a_2+a_3 = a_1+2a_2,\\
a_5 &= a_3+a_4 = 2a_1+3a_2,\\
a_6 &= a_4+a_5 = 3a_1+5a_2,\\
a_7 &= a_5+a_6 = 5a_1+8a_2 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus for each sequence  

\[
\boxed{a_7 = 5a_1+8a_2},\qquad\boxed{b_7 = 5b_1+8b_2}.
\]

Both seventh terms are equal to the same integer \(N\).

---

### 2.  The Diophantine condition

We need two different pairs \((a_1,a_2)\) and \((b_1,b_2)\) of non‑negative
integers satisfying  

\[
\begin{cases}
5a_1+8a_2 = N,\\[2pt]
5b_1+8b_2 = N,\\[2pt]
a_1\le a_2,\; b_1\le b_2,\\[2pt]
a_1\neq b_1 .
\end{cases}
\]

Because \(\gcd(5,8)=1\), any integer \(N\) can be written as \(5x+8y\).
If two different first terms occur, say \(b_1=a_1+8k\) with \(k\ge 1\),
then from the equality of the two expressions for \(N\) we obtain  

\[
5a_1+8a_2 = 5(a_1+8k)+8b_2\Longrightarrow a_2 = b_2+5k .
\]

Hence the two representations are  

\[
(a_1,\;a_2)=(a_1,\;b_2+5k),\qquad
(b_1,\;b_2)=(a_1+8k,\;b_2)
\]

with \(k\ge 1\) and \(b_2\ge 0\).

The monotonicity conditions give  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_1 &\le a_2 = b_2+5k,\\
b_1 = a_1+8k &\le b_2 .
\end{aligned}
\]

From the second inequality we must have  

\[
b_2\ge a_1+8k .
\]

Consequently  

\[
N = 5a_1+8a_2 = 5a_1+8(b_2+5k)
           \ge 5a_1+8(a_1+8k)+40k
           = 13a_1+104k .
\]

To minimise \(N\) we choose the smallest possible \(k\) and \(a_1\):
\(k=1\) and \(a_1=0\).  
Then \(b_2\ge 0+8=8\); taking the minimal \(b_2=8\) gives  

\[
a_2 = b_2+5k = 8+5 = 13,\qquad
b_1 = a_1+8k = 8 .
\]

Thus the two admissible pairs are  

\[
(a_1,a_2)=(0,13),\qquad (b_1,b_2)=(8,8),
\]

and they yield  

\[
N = 5\cdot0+8\cdot13 = 5\cdot8+8\cdot8 = 104 .
\]

Because any other choice forces \(k\ge 1\) and \(a_1\ge 0\), the
inequality \(N\ge 13a_1+104k\) shows that no smaller \(N\) can be
obtained.

