        \[
\begin{aligned}
a_1&=x,\qquad a_2=y\;(x\le y),\\
a_{n}&=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}\;(n\ge3).
\end{aligned}
\]

From the recurrence  

\[
a_3=x+y,\;a_4=x+2y,\;a_5=2x+3y,\;a_6=3x+5y,\;a_7=5x+8y .
\]

Hence the seventh term of any such sequence is  

\[
N=5x+8y\qquad (x,y\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0},\;x\le y).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reformulating the condition

Write \(y\) for the second term.  
From \(N=5x+8y\) we obtain  

\[
x=\frac{N-8y}{5}.
\]

The requirements \(x\ge0\) and \(x\le y\) give  

\[
8y\le N\le13y .
\tag{1}
\]

Because \(x\) must be an integer, \(N-8y\) must be a multiple of \(5\); i.e.  

\[
N\equiv 8y\pmod 5\iff N\equiv 3y\pmod 5 .
\tag{2}
\]

Thus for a fixed \(y\) the admissible values of \(N\) are  

\[
N=8y+5k\qquad (k=0,1,\dots ,y).          \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  When can the same \(N\) arise from two different sequences?

We need two distinct values \(y_1\neq y_2\) for which (3) holds with
some \(k_1\le y_1,\;k_2\le y_2\) and give the same \(N\).
Hence we look for the smallest integer that can be written as  

\[
N=8y_1+5k_1=8y_2+5k_2,\qquad
0\le k_i\le y_i .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Searching the smallest possibility

Listing the numbers obtainable from (3) for successive \(y\):

\[
\begin{array}{c|l}
y & \text{possible }N=8y+5k\\ \hline
0 & 0\\
1 & 8,13\\
2 & 16,21,26\\
3 & 24,29,34,39\\
4 & 32,37,42,47,52\\
5 & 40,45,50,55,60,65\\
6 & 48,53,58,63,68,73,78\\
7 & 56,61,66,71,76,81,86,91\\
8 & 64,69,74,79,84,89,94,99,\mathbf{104}\\
9 & 72,77,82,87,92,97,102,107,112\\
10& 80,85,90,95,100,105,110,115,120,125\\
\vdots &
\end{array}
\]

The first integer that appears for two different \(y\) is  

\[
\boxed{N=104}.
\]

Indeed  

* for \(y=13,\;k=0\): \(N=8\cdot13=104\) giving \(x=\dfrac{104-8\cdot13}{5}=0\);
  the pair \((x,y)=(0,13)\);

* for \(y=8,\;k=8\): \(N=8\cdot8+5\cdot8=104\) giving \(x=\dfrac{104-8\cdot8}{5}=8\);
  the pair \((x,y)=(8,8)\).

Both satisfy \(x\le y\) and have different first terms (\(0\neq8\)).
The corresponding sequences are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
&0,\;13,\;13,\;26,\;39,\;65,\;\mathbf{104},\\
&8,\;8,\;16,\;24,\;40,\;64,\;\mathbf{104},
\end{aligned}
\]

each non‑decreasing, and both have seventh term \(N=104\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimality

For every \(y\le12\) the sets \(\{8y+5k\mid0\le k\le y\}\) are pairwise
disjoint; consequently no integer \(N<104\) can be represented in the
required form by two different \(y\).  Hence no smaller \(N\) works.

