        \[
\begin{cases}
x^{2}-7y^{4}=z,\\[4pt]
7z^{4}+\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{k(2k-1)}}{\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{z-1}\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}}=
\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac1{i(2i-1)}}{\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\frac1{(i+N+1)(2N-i)}},
\qquad N=174545562 .
\end{cases}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Simplifying the fraction  

For any positive integer \(t\),

\[
\frac{1}{t(2t-1)}=-\frac1t+\frac{2}{2t-1},
\]

hence  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{t}\frac1{k(2k-1)}
   =-H_t+2\!\sum_{k=1}^{t}\frac1{2k-1}
   =-H_t+2\bigl(H_{2t}-\tfrac12H_t\bigr)
   =2\bigl(H_{2t}-H_t\bigr),
\tag{1}
\]

where \(H_n\) is the \(n\)-th harmonic number.

For the denominator we write  

\[
\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}
   =\frac{1}{3z+1}\Bigl(\frac1{k+z+1}+\frac1{2z-k}\Bigr),
\]

so  

\[
\sum_{k=0}^{z-1}\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}
   =\frac{2}{3z+1}\sum_{j=z+1}^{2z}\frac1j
   =\frac{2}{3z+1}\bigl(H_{2z}-H_z\bigr).
\tag{2}
\]

Dividing (1) by (2) the factor \(H_{2z}-H_z\) cancels and we obtain the
very simple identity  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle
\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{k(2k-1)}}{\sum_{k=0}^{z-1}\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}}=3z+1 } .
\]

The same computation with the large index \(N=174545562\) gives  

\[
\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac1{i(2i-1)}}{\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\frac1{(i+N+1)(2N-i)}}=3N+1 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The second equation  

Hence the second equation of the system becomes  

\[
7z^{4}+ (3z+1)=3N+1,
\qquad N=174545562 .
\]

Since \(3N+1=523\,636\,687\),

\[
7z^{4}+3z+1=523\,636\,687\Longrightarrow 7z^{4}+3z=523\,636\,686 .
\]

Checking integer values (the left‑hand side grows like \(7z^{4}\)),
the unique integer solution is  

\[
\boxed{z=93}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The first equation  

Now we need integer \(x,y\) such that  

\[
x^{2}=7y^{4}+93 .
\tag{3}
\]

A short search (or a standard descent on the quartic) shows that the only
integer solutions of (3) are  

\[
(y,x)=(\pm1,\pm10),\qquad (y,x)=(\pm7,\pm130).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Maximising \(x+y+z\)

Because \(z=93\) is fixed, we maximise \(x+y\).
Choosing the positive signs gives the two possibilities  

\[
\begin{aligned}
x=10,\;y=1 &\Longrightarrow x+y+z=10+1+93=104,\\[2pt]
x=130,\;y=7 &\Longrightarrow x+y+z=130+7+93=230 .
\end{aligned}
\]

The latter is larger, and any choice with a negative sign would only
decrease the sum.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,\max (x+y+z)=230\,}
\]

