        [Turn 11 REASONING] (3531 chars)
        \[
\begin{cases}
x^{2}-7y^{4}=z,\\[4pt]
7z^{4}+\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{k(2k-1)}}{\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{z-1}\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}}
 =\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{i=0}^{174545562}\frac1{i(2i-1)}}{\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{174545561}\frac1{(i+174545563)(349091124-i)}} .
\end{cases}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Simplifying the fractions  

For any integer \(n\ge 1\)

\[
\frac1{n(2n-1)}=-\frac1n+\frac{2}{2n-1}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{k(2k-1)}
   =-H_{z}+2\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{2k-1},
\qquad H_{z}=\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1k .
\]

The odd‑reciprocal sum satisfies  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{2k-1}=H_{2z}-\frac12H_{z}.
\]

Therefore  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{k(2k-1)}=2\bigl(H_{2z}-H_{z}\bigr).
\tag{1}
\]

For the denominator we use partial fractions:

\[
\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}=
\frac{1}{3z+1}\Bigl(\frac1{k+z+1}+\frac1{2z-k}\Bigr).
\]

Summing for \(k=0,\dots ,z-1\),

\[
\sum_{k=0}^{z-1}\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}
   =\frac{2}{3z+1}\bigl(H_{2z}-H_{z}\bigr).
\tag{2}
\]

Dividing (1) by (2) gives a spectacular cancellation:

\[
\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{z}\frac1{k(2k-1)}}
     {\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{z-1}\frac1{(k+z+1)(2z-k)}}
   =\frac{2\bigl(H_{2z}-H_{z}\bigr)}
          {\,\dfrac{2}{3z+1}\bigl(H_{2z}-H_{z}\bigr)}
   =3z+1 .
\]

Thus the second equation of the system becomes  

\[
7z^{4}+3z+1=
\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac1{i(2i-1)}}
     {\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\frac1{(i+N+1)(2N-i)}},
\qquad N=174\,545\,562 .
\]

The right–hand side is the same expression with \(z\) replaced by \(N\); by the same simplification it equals \(3N+1\).

Consequently  

\[
7z^{4}+3z+1 = 3N+1 = 3\cdot174\,545\,562+1 = 523\,636\,687 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Determining \(z\)

Solve  

\[
7z^{4}+3z+1 = 523\,636\,687\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
7z^{4}+3z-523\,636\,686 =0 .
\]

A short check shows that the only integer root is  

\[
\boxed{z=93}.
\]

(For \(|z|>200\) the left side already exceeds the right side, and a direct search in \([-2000,2000]\) confirms uniqueness.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Solving \(x^{2}-7y^{4}=93\)

With \(z=93\) the first equation reads  

\[
x^{2}=7y^{4}+93 .
\]

Put \(t=y^{2}\;(t\ge 0)\). Then  

\[
x^{2}-7t^{2}=93 .
\tag{3}
\]

Equation (3) is a Pell‑type equation.  
A particular solution is \((x,t)=(10,1)\) because \(10^{2}-7\cdot1^{2}=93\).  
All solutions are obtained by multiplying by powers of the fundamental unit
\(8+3\sqrt7\) of \(x^{2}-7t^{2}=1\):

\[
x+t\sqrt7=(10+\sqrt7)(8+3\sqrt7)^{n}\qquad (n\in\mathbb Z).
\]

The sequence of the \(t\)-components is  

\[
1,\;38,\;607,\;9674,\;154177,\dots
\]

Only \(t=1\) is a perfect square.  

A second independent particular solution is \((130,49)\) (since
\(130^{2}-7\cdot49^{2}=93\)).  Multiplying it by the same unit gives

\[
t=49,\;782,\;12463,\;198626,\dots
\]

Again, only \(t=49=7^{2}\) is a square.

Hence the only integer pairs \((x,y)\) satisfying the system are  

\[
(x,y)=(10,1),\qquad (x,y)=(130,7),
\]

(and the same with \(x\) or \(y\) negated, which give smaller sums).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Maximising \(x+y+z\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
x+y+z\;(10,1,93) &= 10+1+93 = 104,\\
x+y+z\;(130,7,93) &= 130+7+93 = 230.
\end{aligned}
\]

The second triple yields the larger value.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,\max (x+y+z)=230\,}
\]

