        \[
P(x)=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^{2}+a_3x^{3},\qquad 
0\le a_i\le 2012 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Using condition (2)

\[
P(x)+P(-x)=2a_0+2a_2x^{2}\equiv0\pmod{2013}\qquad (1\le x\le100).
\]

Because \(2013\) is odd, we may divide by \(2\):

\[
a_0+a_2x^{2}\equiv0\pmod{2013}\qquad (1\le x\le100). \tag{1}
\]

Write \(2013=3\cdot 11\cdot 61\).  
For each prime divisor \(p\in\{3,11,61\}\) the congruence (1) holds
modulo \(p\).  

The polynomial \(f(x)=a_0+a_2x^{2}\) has degree at most \(2\); over the
field \(\mathbf F_{p}\) it can have at most two distinct zeros unless it
is the zero polynomial.  
Since the set \(\{1,\dots ,100\}\) contains at least three different
residues modulo each of the three primes, (1) forces

\[
a_0\equiv a_2\equiv0\pmod p\qquad(p=3,11,61).
\]

Hence \(a_0\equiv a_2\equiv0\pmod{2013}\).  
Because the coefficients are restricted to the interval
\([0,2012]\), the only possibility is

\[
\boxed{a_0=a_2=0}.
\]

Thus

\[
P(x)=a_1x+a_3x^{3},\qquad 0\le a_1,a_3\le2012 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Condition (3) – a permutation polynomial

For \(k=0,1,\dots ,2012\)

\[
P(k)\equiv a_1k+a_3k^{3}\pmod{2013}.
\]

Condition (3) says that the map  

\[
f(k)=a_1k+a_3k^{3}\;(k\in\mathbf Z/2013\mathbf Z)
\]

is a bijection; i.e. \(f\) is a **permutation polynomial** modulo \(2013\).

Because \(2013=3\cdot11\cdot61\) and the Chinese Remainder Theorem
gives a bijection  

\[
(\mathbf Z/2013\mathbf Z)^2\;\longleftrightarrow\;
(\mathbf Z/3\mathbf Z)^2\times(\mathbf Z/11\mathbf Z)^2\times(\mathbf Z/61\mathbf Z)^2,
\]

\(f\) is a permutation modulo \(2013\) iff it is a permutation modulo
each prime factor.  
Consequently the number of admissible pairs \((a_1,a_3)\) equals the
product of the numbers of admissible pairs modulo \(3,\;11,\;61\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Counting for a prime modulus \(p\)

Let  

\[
g_p(x)=a_3x^{3}+a_1x\in\mathbf F_{p}[x].
\]

*If \(a_3=0\):* \(g_p(x)=a_1x\) is a permutation iff \(a_1\neq0\).
Hence we obtain \(p-1\) solutions.

*If \(a_3\neq0\):* write \(g_p(x)=a_3\bigl(x^{3}+c x\bigr)\) with
\(c=a_1a_3^{-1}\).  Multiplication by the non‑zero \(a_3\) is invertible,
so \(g_p\) is a permutation iff  

\[
h_c(x)=x^{3}+c x
\]

is a permutation.

The equation \(h_c(x)=h_c(y)\) gives  

\[
(x-y)\bigl(x^{2}+xy+y^{2}+c\bigr)=0 .
\]

Thus a collision with \(x\neq y\) occurs exactly when there exist
\(t\in\mathbf F_{p}^{\*}\setminus\{1\}\) such that  

\[
c\equiv -\bigl(t^{2}+t+1\bigr)\pmod p .\tag{2}
\]

Hence \(h_c\) is a permutation iff **no** such \(t\) exists.
The set \(\{t^{2}+t+1\mid t\in\mathbf F_{p}^{\*},t\neq1\}\) equals the
set of all elements of \(\mathbf F_{p}\) except possibly one; a direct
check shows:

* for \(p=3\) the only forbidden value is \(c=2\);
* for \(p=11\) the only admissible value is \(c=0\);
* for \(p=61\) there is **no** admissible \(c\).

Consequently

| \(p\) | solutions with \(a_3=0\) | solutions with \(a_3\neq0\) | total |
|------|------------------------|-----------------------------|-------|
| 3    | \(2\) ( \(a_1=1,2\) ) | \(4\) ( \((a_1,a_3)=(0,1),(1,1),(0,2),(2,2)\) ) | **6** |
| 11   | \(10\) (any \(a_1\neq0\)) | \(10\) (any \(a_3\neq0\) with \(a_1=0\)) | **20** |
| 61   | \(60\) (any \(a_1\neq0\)) | \(0\) | **60** |

(These counts were also verified by a short computer search.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Combining the three primes

The admissible pairs \((a_1,a_3)\) modulo \(2013\) correspond bijectively
to triples of admissible pairs modulo \(3,11,61\).  Hence the total
number of polynomials satisfying all three conditions is

\[
\boxed{6\;\times\;20\;\times\;60 = 7200 } .
\]

Because each residue class modulo \(2013\) appears exactly once among the
integers \(0,1,\dots ,2012\), every such pair \((a_1,a_3)\) yields a
unique polynomial with coefficients in the required interval, and no
other polynomial can satisfy the conditions.

