        [Turn 14 REASONING] (3350 chars)
        \[
P(x)=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^{2}+a_3x^{3},\qquad 
0\le a_i\le 2012 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Using condition (2)

\[
P(x)+P(-x)=2(a_0+a_2x^{2}) .
\]

For every integer \(1\le x\le100\)

\[
2(a_0+a_2x^{2})\equiv0\pmod{2013}\Longrightarrow 
a_0+a_2x^{2}\equiv0\pmod{2013}.
\]

Hence for all \(x,y\in\{1,\dots ,100\}\)

\[
a_2(x^{2}-y^{2})\equiv0\pmod{2013}.
\]

The set \(\{x^{2}-y^{2}\mid 1\le x,y\le100\}\) generates the whole
ideal \((1)\) of \(\mathbb Z_{2013}\); indeed

\[
\operatorname{lcm}_{x,y}\frac{2013}{\gcd(2013,x^{2}-y^{2})}=2013 .
\]

Consequently \(a_2\equiv0\pmod{2013}\), and because \(0\le a_2\le2012\)
we obtain  

\[
a_2=0,\qquad a_0=0 .
\]

Thus every admissible polynomial has the form  

\[
\boxed{P(x)=a_1x+a_3x^{3}},\qquad a_1,a_3\in\{0,1,\dots ,2012\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Condition (3) – a permutation polynomial

Condition (3) says that the residues  

\[
P(0),P(1),\dots ,P(2012)\pmod{2013}
\]

are all different; i.e. the map  

\[
f:\mathbb Z_{2013}\longrightarrow\mathbb Z_{2013},\qquad 
f(x)=a_1x+a_3x^{3}
\]

is a **permutation** of the ring \(\mathbb Z_{2013}\).

Because  

\[
2013=3\cdot11\cdot61
\]

is square‑free, a polynomial is a permutation modulo \(2013\) iff it is a
permutation modulo each prime factor (Chinese Remainder Theorem).  
Hence the number of admissible pairs \((a_1,a_3)\) equals

\[
N_{3}\;N_{11}\;N_{61},
\]

where \(N_{p}\) is the number of pairs \((a,b)\in\mathbb F_{p}^{2}\) for
which \(x\mapsto ax+bx^{3}\) permutes \(\mathbb F_{p}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Counting \(N_{p}\) for a prime \(p\)

*If \(p>3\).*  
Write \(f(x)=ax+bx^{3}=x\,(a+bx^{2})\).

*   If \(a\neq0,\;b=0\) we have the linear permutation \(x\mapsto ax\);
    there are \(p-1\) possibilities.

*   If \(a=0,\;b\neq0\) we obtain the monomial \(bx^{3}\).  
    This is a permutation iff \(\gcd(3,p-1)=1\), i.e. iff \(p\equiv2\pmod3\);
    when it occurs we again have \(p-1\) possibilities (any non‑zero \(b\)).

*   If \(a\neq0,\;b\neq0\) then \(f\) cannot be a permutation.
    Indeed, \(f(x)=f(y)\) with \(x\neq y\) is equivalent to  

    \[
    (x-y)\bigl(a+b(x^{2}+xy+y^{2})\bigr)=0 .
    \]

    The quadratic factor always has a solution in \(\mathbb F_{p}\) for
    \(p>3\), giving a collision.  Hence no mixed term works.

Therefore, for \(p>3\)

\[
N_{p}= \begin{cases}
2(p-1), & p\equiv2\pmod3,\\[2mm]
(p-1),  & p\equiv1\pmod3 .
\end{cases}
\]

*For \(p=3\).*  A direct check of the \(3^{2}=9\) possibilities gives  

\[
N_{3}=6 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Applying to the three prime factors

\[
\begin{aligned}
p=3 &: \; N_{3}=6,\\
p=11&: 11\equiv2\pmod3\;\Longrightarrow\; N_{11}=2(11-1)=20,\\
p=61&: 61\equiv1\pmod3\;\Longrightarrow\; N_{61}=61-1=60.
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Total number of polynomials

Each residue class modulo \(2013\) appears exactly once among the integers
\(0,1,\dots ,2012\); consequently every pair \((a_1,a_3)\) counted above
gives a unique polynomial with coefficients in the required interval.
Hence

\[
\boxed{\,6\cdot20\cdot60 = 7200\,}
\]

polynomials satisfy all three conditions.

