        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{For a fixed }n,\;S=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i,\qquad 
T=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Bigl|\frac{S-a_i}{n}-a_i\Bigr|
   =\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl|S-(n+1)a_i\bigr|.
\end{aligned}
\]

The function \(g(\mathbf a)=\sum_{i}|S-(n+1)a_i|\) is convex in each \(a_i\) (absolute value of an affine function).  
On the hyper‑cube \([-1,1]^n\) a convex function attains its maximum at an extreme point; therefore each
\(a_i\) can be taken to be \(\pm1\).

---

### 1.  Even \(n=2k\)

Choose exactly \(k\) of the \(a_i\)’s equal to \(-1\) and the other \(k\) equal to \(1\).  
Then \(S=0\) and for every \(i\)

\[
|S-(n+1)a_i|=|(2k+1)(\pm1)|=2k+1=n+1 .
\]

Hence  

\[
f(n)=\frac1n\cdot n (n+1)=n+1 .
\]

---

### 2.  Odd \(n=2k+1\)

Take \(k+1\) of the numbers equal to \(-1\) and the remaining \(k\) equal to \(1\).  
Now \(S=-1\).  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{if }a_i=-1 &: |S-(n+1)a_i| = |-1-(2k+2)(-1)|
                     =2k+1=n ,\\[2mm]
\text{if }a_i=1 &: |S-(n+1)a_i| = |-1-(2k+2)|
                     =2k+3=n+2 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus  

\[
\sum_{i}|S-(n+1)a_i|
   =(k+1)n+k(n+2)=n(n+1)-1,
\]

and  

\[
f(n)=\frac{n(n+1)-1}{n}=n+1-\frac1n .
\]

No other choice of the \(a_i\)’s can give a larger value, so the maximum is attained at the
configurations above.

Consequently  

\[
f(n)=
\begin{cases}
n+1, & n\text{ even},\\[2mm]
n+1-\dfrac1n, & n\text{ odd}.
\end{cases}
\]

---

### 3.  Computing \(\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{225} kf(k)\)

\[
k\,f(k)=
\begin{cases}
k(k+1), & k\text{ even},\\[2mm]
k(k+1)-1, & k\text{ odd}.
\end{cases}
\]

Hence  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{225} kf(k)=\sum_{k=1}^{225}\bigl(k^2+k\bigr)-\#\{\text{odd }k\le225\}.
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=1}^{225}k^2 &=\frac{225\cdot226\cdot451}{6}=3\,822\,225,\\
\sum_{k=1}^{225}k   &=\frac{225\cdot226}{2}=25\,425,\\
\#\{\text{odd }k\le225\} &=\frac{225+1}{2}=113.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{225} kf(k)=3\,822\,225+25\,425-113=3\,847\,537.
\]

\[
\boxed{3847537}
\]

