---

### 1.  Reduction to two variables  

Let  

\[
A=\sum_{i=1}^{5}x_i,\qquad t=x_5 .
\]

Because the numbers are ordered,
\[
\frac{A-t}{4}\le t\le\frac{1-A}{4}.
\]

The configuration that minimises the sum of squares for given \(A,t\) is  

\[
x_1=x_2=x_3=x_4=\frac{A-t}{4},\qquad
x_5=t,\qquad
x_6=x_7=x_8=x_9=\frac{1-A}{4}.
\]

For this configuration  

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_1&=\sum_{i=1}^{9}|x_i-t|
      =1+t-2A,\\[2mm]
Q&=\sum_{i=1}^{9}x_i^{2}
   =\frac{(A-t)^2+(1-A)^2}{4}+t^{2},\\[2mm]
S_2&=\sum_{1\le i<j\le 9}(x_i-x_j)^2
    =9Q-1 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence the expression to be maximised becomes  

\[
F(A,t)=\frac{(1+t-2A)^{2}\bigl(9Q-1\bigr)}{Q^{3}},
\qquad 
Q=\frac{(A-t)^{2}+(1-A)^{2}}{4}+t^{2},
\]
with the admissible region  

\[
\boxed{A\le 5t\quad\text{and}\quad A\le 1-4t } .
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  Behaviour of the factor depending only on \(Q\)

\[
g(Q)=\frac{9Q-1}{Q^{3}},\qquad 
g'(Q)=\frac{3(1-6Q)}{Q^{4}} .
\]

Thus \(g\) increases up to \(Q=\frac16\) and decreases afterwards.
The maximal possible value of \(g\) is  

\[
g\!\left(\frac16\right)=108 .
\tag{2}
\]

Consequently, at the optimum we must have  

\[
Q=\frac16 .
\tag{3}
\]

---

### 3.  Solving the system \(Q=\frac16\) together with the boundary condition  

From (3),

\[
\frac{(A-t)^{2}+(1-A)^{2}}{4}+t^{2}= \frac16 .
\tag{4}
\]

Because the feasible region (1) is a convex cone, the maximum of
\(F\) will be attained on the boundary where the two linear constraints are equal, i.e.

\[
A=5t\qquad\text{or}\qquad A=1-4t .
\tag{5}
\]

We treat the two possibilities.

---

#### 3.1  Case \(A=5t\)

Insert \(A=5t\) into (4):

\[
\frac{(5t-t)^{2}+(1-5t)^{2}}{4}+t^{2}= \frac{(4t)^{2}+(1-5t)^{2}}{4}+t^{2}= \frac{16t^{2}+(1-5t)^{2}}{4}+t^{2}= \frac16 .
\]

Solving gives  

\[
t=\frac{1}{9},\qquad A=\frac{5}{9}.
\]

For these values  

\[
S_{1}=1+t-2A=1+\frac19- \frac{10}{9}=0 .
\]

Hence \(F=0\); this cannot be the maximum.

---

#### 3.2  Case \(A=1-4t\)

Insert \(A=1-4t\) into (4):

\[
\frac{((1-4t)-t)^{2}+(1-(1-4t))^{2}}{4}+t^{2}
   =\frac{(1-5t)^{2}+(4t)^{2}}{4}+t^{2}= \frac16 .
\]

This is the same equation as in the previous case, therefore it yields the
same solution  

\[
t=\frac19,\qquad A=1-\frac{4}{9}= \frac{5}{9}.
\]

Now  

\[
S_{1}=1+t-2A = 1+\frac19- \frac{10}{9}=0 .
\]

Again \(F=0\).

Thus the optimum does **not** lie on the lines where the two linear
constraints are simultaneously active.  
The maximum must therefore be attained inside the region where only one
of the inequalities in (1) is active.

---

### 4.  Interior optimum – solving the two‑equation system  

We keep the condition \(Q=\frac16\) (which gives the maximal factor
\(g(Q)=108\)) and treat \(A\) as a free variable, using only the
inequality that keeps the ordering, e.g. \(A\le 5t\).  
From (4) we express \(t\) in terms of \(A\).

\[
\frac{(A-t)^{2}+(1-A)^{2}}{4}+t^{2}= \frac16
\Longrightarrow
(A-t)^{2}+4t^{2}= \frac23-(1-A)^{2}.
\]

Expanding and collecting terms in \(t\):

\[
A^{2}-2At+t^{2}+4t^{2}= \frac23-(1-2A+A^{2})
\]
\[
5t^{2}-2At = \frac23-(1-2A+A^{2})-A^{2}
          = \frac23-1+2A
          = 2A-\frac13 .
\]

Hence  

\[
5t^{2}-2At-2A+\frac13 =0 .
\tag{6}
\]

Treat (6) as a quadratic in \(t\):

\[
t=\frac{2A\pm\sqrt{4A^{2}+20\bigl(2A-\frac13\bigr)}}{10}
   =\frac{A\pm\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}}}{5}.
\tag{7}
\]

Because the numbers are ordered we need \(t\ge\frac{A-t}{4}\), i.e.
\(A\le5t\). Substituting (7) into this inequality selects the “+’’ sign
in (7). Therefore

\[
t=\frac{A+\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}}}{5},
\qquad\text{with }A\ge -\frac{1}{5}\text{ (to keep the radicand non‑negative)}.
\]

Now \(S_{1}=1+t-2A\). Using the expression of \(t\),

\[
S_{1}=1+\frac{A+\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}}}{5}-2A
      =\frac{5-9A+\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}}}{5}.
\]

Hence  

\[
F(A)=108\; \frac{S_{1}^{2}}{Q^{3}} =108\;S_{1}^{2}\;(6)^{3}
      =108\cdot 216\; S_{1}^{2}
      =23328\;S_{1}^{2},
\]
because for the optimum we have \(Q=\frac16\) and therefore \(Q^{3}= \frac{1}{216}\).

Consequently maximising \(F\) is equivalent to maximising \(S_{1}\).

So we maximise  

\[
S_{1}(A)=\frac{5-9A+\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}}}{5},
\qquad A\le 5t .
\]

Insert the expression of \(t\) into the inequality \(A\le5t\):

\[
A\le5\Bigl(\frac{A+\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}}}{5}\Bigr)
\Longrightarrow
A\le A+\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}},
\]

which is always true (the square‑root is non‑negative).  
Thus the only remaining restriction is the non‑negativity of the radicand:

\[
A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}\ge0
\Longrightarrow
A\ge\frac{-10+\sqrt{100+\frac{8}{3}}}{2}
   =\frac{-10+\sqrt{\frac{308}{3}}}{2}
   \approx -0.199 .
\]

Hence \(A\) can be any number larger than this bound.  
Since \(S_{1}(A)\) is decreasing for large \(A\) (the term \(-9A\) dominates),
the maximum occurs at the smallest admissible \(A\), i.e. when the
radicand is zero:

\[
A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}=0
\Longrightarrow
A=-5+\sqrt{25+\frac{2}{3}}
   =-5+\sqrt{\frac{77}{3}}
   =-5+\frac{\sqrt{231}}{3}.
\]

For this \(A\),

\[
\sqrt{A^{2}+10A-\frac{2}{3}}=0,
\qquad
t=\frac{A}{5}.
\]

Thus  

\[
t=\frac{-5+\frac{\sqrt{231}}{3}}{5}
   =-1+\frac{\sqrt{231}}{15}.
\]

Now compute \(S_{1}\):

\[
S_{1}=1+t-2A
     =1+\Bigl(-1+\frac{\sqrt{231}}{15}\Bigr)-2\Bigl(-5+\frac{\sqrt{231}}{3}\Bigr)
     =\frac{\sqrt{231}}{15}+10-\frac{2\sqrt{231}}{3}
     =10-\frac{9\sqrt{231}}{15}
     =10-\frac{3\sqrt{231}}{5}.
\]

Numerically,

\[
\sqrt{231}\approx15.19868,\qquad
S_{1}\approx10-\frac{3\cdot15.19868}{5}
          =10-9.11921\approx0.88079 .
\]

Finally

\[
F_{\max}=23328\,S_{1}^{2}
        =23328\bigl(10-\frac{3\sqrt{231}}{5}\bigr)^{2}.
\]

Carrying out the square:

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_{1}^{2}
&= \left(10-\frac{3\sqrt{231}}{5}\right)^{2}
 =100- \frac{60}{5}\sqrt{231}+ \frac{9\cdot231}{25}\\
&=100-12\sqrt{231}+ \frac{2079}{25}
 =100-12\sqrt{231}+83.16\\
&=183.16-12\sqrt{231}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Exactly,

\[
S_{1}^{2}= \frac{2500-120\sqrt{231}+2079}{25}
        =\frac{4579-120\sqrt{231}}{25}.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{F_{\max}=23328\cdot\frac{4579-120\sqrt{231}}{25}
      =\frac{23328}{25}\bigl(4579-120\sqrt{231}\bigr)}.
\]

Simplifying the rational factor:

\[
\frac{23328}{25}= \frac{2^{5}\cdot3^{3}\cdot 7}{25}= \frac{23328}{25}.
\]

Hence the maximal value is

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle
\frac{23328\,(4579-120\sqrt{231})}{25}}
\approx 1.628\times10^{4}.
\]

