        \[
M_n(\lambda)=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(i^{3}-i\bigr)_{i=2}^{n}-\lambda\,\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\!T},
\qquad 
\mathbf 1=(1,\dots ,1)^{T}\in\mathbb R^{\,n-1}.
\]

For a diagonal matrix \(A=\operatorname{diag}(a_i)\) the matrix determinant lemma gives  

\[
\det(A-\lambda\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\!T})
   =\det A\Bigl(1-\lambda\,\mathbf 1^{T}A^{-1}\mathbf 1\Bigr).
\]

Here \(a_i=i^{3}-i\;(i\ge 2)\) are all positive, so \(A\) is invertible and  

\[
\mathbf 1^{T}A^{-1}\mathbf 1
   =\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i^{3}-i}.
\]

Hence \(\det M_n(\lambda)=0\) iff  

\[
1-\lambda\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i^{3}-i}=0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\lambda_n=\frac1{\displaystyle\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i^{3}-i}} .
\]

---

### Evaluating the sum

\[
\frac1{i^{3}-i}= \frac1{i(i-1)(i+1)}
               =\frac1{2(i+1)}+\frac1{2(i-1)}-\frac1{i},
\]
so

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i^{3}-i}
&=\frac12\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i+1}
  +\frac12\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i-1}
  -\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i}\\[2mm]
&=\frac12\Bigl(H_{n+1}-\tfrac32\Bigr)+\frac12 H_{\,n-1}
   -\bigl(H_n-1\bigr)\\[2mm]
&=\frac14-\frac1{2n(n+1)},
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(H_m=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\frac1k\) is the harmonic number.

Thus  

\[
\lambda_n
   =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac14-\frac1{2n(n+1)}}
   =\frac{4n(n+1)}{n^{2}+n-2}.
\]

---

### Limit superior

\[
\lambda_n
   =\frac{4n^{2}+4n}{n^{2}+n-2}
   =4\;\frac{1+1/n}{1+1/n-2/n^{2}}
   \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}4.
\]

The sequence \(\lambda_n\) is decreasing for \(n\ge2\) (e.g. \(6,4.8,4.44,\dots\)), so its lim sup equals its limit.

\[
\boxed{I=4}
\]

