        \[
M_n(\lambda)=\operatorname{diag}(2^3-2,\dots ,n^3-n)-\lambda J_{n-1},
\qquad J_{n-1}= \mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\!T}
\]

where \(\mathbf 1\) is the column vector of ones of length \(n-1\).
Hence  

\[
\det\bigl(M_n(\lambda)\bigr)=\det(D-\lambda J_{n-1}),
\qquad D=\operatorname{diag}(i^3-i)_{i=2}^{n}.
\]

Using the matrix‑determinant lemma  

\[
\det(D+u v^{\!T})=\det(D)\bigl(1+v^{\!T}D^{-1}u\bigr),
\]

with \(u=-\lambda\mathbf 1,\;v=\mathbf 1\), we obtain  

\[
\det(M_n(\lambda))
 =\Bigl(\prod_{i=2}^{n}(i^3-i)\Bigr)
   \Bigl(1-\lambda\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i^3-i}\Bigr).
\]

Since the product is non‑zero, \(\det(M_n(\lambda))=0\) iff  

\[
1-\lambda\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i^3-i}=0
\Longrightarrow
\boxed{\displaystyle \lambda_n=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac{1}{i^3-i}} } .
\]

Thus \(\lambda_n\) is uniquely determined and is the maximal \(\lambda\) with zero determinant.

---

### Evaluating the series

\[
\frac{1}{i^3-i}= \frac{1}{i(i-1)(i+1)}
            =\frac{1}{2(i-1)}-\frac{1}{i}+\frac{1}{2(i+1)} .
\]

Hence for \(N\ge2\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_N&:=\sum_{i=2}^{N}\frac{1}{i^3-i}\\
&=\sum_{i=2}^{N}\Bigl(\frac{1}{2(i-1)}-\frac{1}{i}+\frac{1}{2(i+1)}\Bigr)\\
&=\frac{(N-1)(N+2)}{4N(N+1)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Taking \(N\to\infty\),

\[
\sum_{i=2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{i^3-i}
   =\lim_{N\to\infty}S_N
   =\frac14 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{S_n}\downarrow \frac{1}{\frac14}=4 .
\]

Because \(\lambda_n\) is decreasing and convergent,  

\[
\limsup_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n=\lim_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n=4 .
\]

\[
\boxed{I=4}
\]

