\]

Thus \(\det(M_n(\lambda))=0\) iff  

\[
\lambda=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i^{3}-i}}
\equiv\lambda_n .
\]

Hence \(\lambda_n\) is the unique (and therefore maximal) root.

---

### Evaluating the sum

\[
\frac1{i^{3}-i}= \frac1{i(i-1)(i+1)}
          =\frac{1}{2(i-1)}-\frac1{i}+\frac{1}{2(i+1)} .
\]

Summing from \(i=2\) to \(n\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_n&=\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i^{3}-i}\\[2mm]
   &=\frac12\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i-1}
     -\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i}
     +\frac12\sum_{i=2}^{n}\frac1{i+1}\\[2mm]
   &=\frac12 H_{\,n-1}
     -\bigl(H_n-1\bigr)
     +\frac12\bigl(H_{\,n+1}-\tfrac32\bigr)\\[2mm]
   &=\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)},
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(H_m\) denotes the \(m\)-th harmonic number.  
Consequently

\[
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{S_n}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)}}
        =\frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}.
\]

---

### Limit superior

\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n
   =\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}=4 .
\]

Since the sequence \(\lambda_n\) is decreasing for \(n\ge2\) (e.g. \(6,4.8,4.44,\dots\)), its limsup equals its limit.

\[
