        [Turn 4 REASONING] (2870 chars)
        \[
S=\Bigl\{\frac{2y-3}{2x-1}\;\Big|\; \min\{|x-2|,|x+2|\}+|y|\le 1\Bigr\}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The domain in the \((x,y)\)-plane  

\[
\min\{|x-2|,|x+2|\}+|y|\le 1
\]

means that the point \((x,y)\) is at \(L^{1}\)-distance at most \(1\) from **the nearer of the two points**
\((2,0)\) and \((-2,0)\).  
Hence the domain is the union of two diamonds (rhombuses) of radius \(1\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
D_{+}&=\{(x,y):|x-2|+|y|\le 1\},\\[2mm]
D_{-}&=\{(x,y):|x+2|+|y|\le 1\}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Their vertices are  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
D_{+}&(3,0),\;(1,0),\;(2,1),\;(2,-1)\\ \hline
D_{-}&(-3,0),\;(-1,0),\;(-2,1),\;(-2,-1)
\end{array}
\]

The two diamonds are disjoint; the gap \((-1,1)\) in the \(x\)‑direction guarantees that
\(2x-1\neq0\) on the whole domain, so the expression is well defined.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Behaviour of the function on an edge  

Each edge of a diamond is a line of slope \(\pm1\); write it as  

\[
y=ax+b,\qquad a=\pm1 .
\]

On such an edge  

\[
f(x)=\frac{2y-3}{2x-1}= \frac{2ax+2b-3}{2x-1}.
\]

Its derivative is  

\[
f'(x)=\frac{2a(2x-1)-2(2ax+2b-3)}{(2x-1)^{2}}
      =\frac{3-a-2b}{(2x-1)^{2}} .
\]

Thus \(f'(x)\) is constant on the whole edge.  
If \(3-a-2b\neq0\) the function is monotone on that edge, so the extreme
values are attained at the edge’s endpoints.  
If \(3-a-2b=0\) then \(f\) is constant on the edge.

Checking the four edges of each diamond, only the edge joining
\((-1,0)\) and \((-2,-1)\) (the line \(y=x+1\)) satisfies
\(3-a-2b=0\); there \(f\equiv1\).  
All other edges are monotone, hence the extreme values of \(f\) on each
diamond occur at its vertices.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Values at the vertices  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
(x,y) & f(x,y)=\dfrac{2y-3}{2x-1}\\ \hline
(3,0) & -\dfrac35\\
(1,0) & -3\\
(2,1) & -\dfrac13\\
(2,-1)& -\dfrac53\\[2mm]
(-3,0)& \dfrac37\\
(-1,0)& 1\\
(-2,1)& \dfrac15\\
(-2,-1)& 1
\end{array}
\]

For the right‑hand diamond \(D_{+}\) the smallest and largest values are

\[
\min_{D_{+}}f=-3,\qquad \max_{D_{+}}f=-\frac13,
\]

so  

\[
f(D_{+})=[-3,\,-\tfrac13].
\]

For the left‑hand diamond \(D_{-}\)

\[
\min_{D_{-}}f=\frac15,\qquad \max_{D_{-}}f=1,
\]

hence  

\[
f(D_{-})=[\tfrac15,\,1].
\]

The two intervals are disjoint; therefore

\[
S=[-3,\,-\tfrac13]\;\cup\;[\tfrac15,\,1].
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Length of \(S\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{length}(S)&=
\Bigl(-\frac13-(-3)\Bigr)+\Bigl(1-\frac15\Bigr)\\[2mm]
&=\frac{8}{3}+\frac{4}{5}
   =\frac{40}{15}+\frac{12}{15}
   =\frac{52}{15}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The fraction \(\dfrac{52}{15}\) is already in lowest terms
(\(\gcd(52,15)=1\)).  

Thus \(m=52,\;p=15\) and  

\[
\boxed{m+p=52+15=67}.
\]

