        [Turn 5 REASONING] (3585 chars)
        \[
S=\Bigl\{\frac{2y-3}{2x-1}\;\Big|\;
\min\{|x-2|,|x+2|\}+|y|\le 1\Bigr\}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The admissible region in the \((x,y)\)-plane  

Put  

\[
d(x)=\min\{|x-2|,|x+2|\}.
\]

The condition is \(d(x)+|y|\le 1\); hence for a fixed \(x\)

\[
|y|\le 1-d(x)\qquad\text{(so we need }d(x)\le 1\text{)} .
\]

Because the two points \(-2\) and \(2\) are distance \(4\) apart,
\(d(x)\le 1\) only when \(x\) lies within distance \(1\) of one of them.
Thus  

\[
x\in[-3,-1]\cup[1,3].
\]

For these \(x\) we have  

\[
h(x)=1-d(x)=\begin{cases}
x+3, & -3\le x\le -2,\\[2mm]
-\,x-1, & -2\le x\le -1,\\[2mm]
x-1, & 1\le x\le 2,\\[2mm]
3-x, & 2\le x\le 3,
\end{cases}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad |y|\le h(x).
\]

Hence the admissible set consists of two congruent isosceles triangles
with vertices \((-3,0),(-2,\pm1),(-1,0)\) and \((1,0),(2,\pm1),(3,0)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The function  

\[
f(x,y)=\frac{2y-3}{2x-1},
\qquad D(x)=2x-1 .
\]

For a fixed \(x\) the function is linear in \(y\):
\[
f(x,y)=\frac{2}{D(x)}\,y-\frac{3}{D(x)} .
\]

*If \(D(x)<0\) (the left triangle) the function decreases with \(y\);*  
*if \(D(x)>0\) (the right triangle) it increases with \(y\).*

Consequently, for each \(x\) the image of the vertical segment
\(|y|\le h(x)\) is an interval whose endpoints are obtained at
\(y=\pm h(x)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  End‑point formulas  

Denote  

\[
f_{\min}(x)=\frac{2h(x)-3}{D(x)},\qquad
f_{\max}(x)=\frac{-2h(x)-3}{D(x)} .
\]

Because the sign of \(D(x)\) changes, the actual minimum and maximum on
the left side are swapped:

* left side \((x\in[-3,-1])\)

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
x\text{-interval}&h(x)&
\begin{array}{c}
\text{minimum}\\ \text{maximum}
\end{array}\\ \hline
[-3,-2] & x+3 &
\displaystyle\Bigl[\frac{2x+3}{2x-1},\; \frac{-2x-9}{2x-1}\Bigr]\\[2mm]
[-2,-1] & -x-1 &
\displaystyle\Bigl[\frac{-2x-5}{2x-1},\;1\Bigr]
\end{array}
\]

* right side \((x\in[1,3])\)

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
x\text{-interval}&h(x)&
\begin{array}{c}
\text{minimum}\\ \text{maximum}
\end{array}\\ \hline
[1,2] & x-1 &
\displaystyle\Bigl[\frac{-2x-1}{2x-1},\; \frac{2x-5}{2x-1}\Bigr]\\[2mm]
[2,3] & 3-x &
\displaystyle\Bigl[\frac{2x-9}{2x-1},\; \frac{3-2x}{2x-1}\Bigr]
\end{array}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Extreme values on each component  

All the above functions are monotone on their intervals, so the extreme
values are obtained at the endpoints.

*Left triangle*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min_{[-3,-1]}f &=\frac{2(-2)+3}{2(-2)-1}= \frac{-1}{-5}= \frac15,\\[2mm]
\max_{[-3,-1]}f &=1 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the image of the left component is the whole interval  

\[
\boxed{[\,\tfrac15,\;1\,]} .
\]

*Right triangle*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min_{[1,3]}f &=\frac{-2\cdot1-1}{2\cdot1-1}= -3,\\[2mm]
\max_{[1,3]}f &=\frac{2\cdot2-5}{2\cdot2-1}= -\frac13 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence the image of the right component is  

\[
\boxed{[\, -3,\; -\tfrac13 \,]} .
\]

The two intervals are disjoint; the set \(S\) is their union.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Length of \(S\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
|S| &=\bigl(-\tfrac13-(-3)\bigr)+\bigl(1-\tfrac15\bigr)\\[2mm]
    &=\frac{8}{3}+\frac{4}{5}
      =\frac{40}{15}+\frac{12}{15}
      =\frac{52}{15}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The fraction \(\dfrac{52}{15}\) is already in lowest terms
(\(\gcd(52,15)=1\)).  

Therefore \(m=52,\;p=15\) and  

\[
\boxed{m+p=52+15=67}.
\]

