        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Let the chord }CD\text{ lie on the }x\text{-axis with }R=(0,0),\;C=(-4,0),\;D=(6,0).\\
&\text{The centre }O\text{ of }\Omega\text{ lies on the perpendicular bisector of }CD,
\;O=(1,h),\;R_0^2=25+h^2 .
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 1.  Centres of \(\Omega_1,\Omega_2\)

\(\Omega_1\) passes through \(C,R\); its centre \(O_1\) is on the perpendicular bisector of \(CR\):
\[
O_1=(-2,y_1),\qquad r_1=|O_1C|=\sqrt{4+y_1^{2}} .
\]

\(\Omega_2\) passes through \(D,R\); its centre \(O_2\) is on the perpendicular bisector of \(DR\):
\[
O_2=(3,y_2),\qquad r_2=|O_2D|=\sqrt{9+y_2^{2}} .
\]

Both circles are internally tangent to \(\Omega\); therefore  

\[
|OO_1|=R_0-r_1,\qquad |OO_2|=R_0-r_2 .
\]

Squaring and simplifying gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
(1+2)^2+(h-y_1)^2&=(\sqrt{25+h^2}-\sqrt{4+y_1^2})^2\\
\Longrightarrow\;&4h^{2}-20hy_1+25y_1^{2}=0
                =(-2h+5y_1)^2,
\end{aligned}
\]
hence \(y_1=\dfrac{2}{5}h\).

Similarly  

\[
9h^{2}-30hy_2+25y_2^{2}=0
      =(-3h+5y_2)^2,
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad y_2=\dfrac{3}{5}h .
\]

---

### 2.  Using the given chord \(UV\)

The line \(RS\) is the radical axis of \(\Omega_1\) and \(\Omega_2\); it is perpendicular to \(O_1O_2\) and passes through \(R\).
Thus a direction vector of \(RS\) is  

\[
\mathbf v=\bigl(-(y_2-y_1),\,5\bigr)=(y_1-y_2,5).
\]

Let the line be \((x,y)=t\mathbf v\).  
Its intersection with \(\Omega\) satisfies  

\[
(x-1)^2+(y-h)^2=R_0^{2}=25+h^{2}.
\]

Substituting \(x=at,\;y=bt\) (\(a=y_1-y_2,\;b=5\)) gives the quadratic  

\[
(a^{2}+b^{2})t^{2}-2(a+bh)t-24=0 .
\]

If \(t_1,t_2\) are the two solutions, the chord length is  

\[
UV=|t_1-t_2|\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}
      =\sqrt{\frac{D}{a^{2}+b^{2}}},\qquad
D=4\bigl[(a+bh)^{2}+24(a^{2}+b^{2})\bigr].
\]

Since \(UV=11\),

\[
\frac{4(a+bh)^{2}}{a^{2}+b^{2}}=25
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\bigl((y_1-y_2)+5h\bigr)^{2}
      =\frac{25}{4}\bigl((y_1-y_2)^{2}+25\bigr). \tag{1}
\]

Insert \(y_1=\frac25h,\;y_2=\frac35h\) into (1).  
After simplification we obtain  

\[
\frac{2279}{100}h^{2}= \frac{625}{4}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
h^{2}= \frac{15625}{2279}.
\]

Consequently  

\[
y_1=\frac{2}{5}h=\frac{50}{\sqrt{2279}},\qquad
y_2=\frac{3}{5}h=\frac{75}{\sqrt{2279}} .
\]

