### 3.  Length \(RS\)

For the intersecting circles \(\Omega_1,\Omega_2\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
r_1^{2}&=4+y_1^{2}= \frac{11616}{2279},\\
r_2^{2}&=9+y_2^{2}= \frac{26136}{2279},\\
d^{2}&=|O_1O_2|^{2}=5^{2}+(y_2-y_1)^{2}= \frac{57600}{2279},
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(d=|O_1O_2|\).

Let \(a\) be the distance from \(O_1\) to the midpoint \(M\) of the common chord \(RS\):
\[
a=\frac{r_1^{2}-r_2^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}
   =\frac{359}{4\sqrt{2279}} .
\]

The half‑length of the chord is  

\[
\frac{RS}{2}= \sqrt{r_1^{2}-a^{2}}
            =\sqrt{\frac{56975}{16\cdot2279}} .
\]

Hence  

\[
RS^{2}= \frac{56975}{4\cdot2279}
      =\frac{25}{4}.
\]

Thus \(RS^{2}= \dfrac{25}{4}\), so \(m=25,\;n=4\) and  

\[
\boxed{m+n=29}.
\]

