        [Turn 9 REASONING] (3305 chars)
        Let the centre of the given circle \(\Omega\) be \(O\) and let its radius be \(R\).
Place the chord \(CD\) horizontally with its midpoint on the \(y\)-axis.

\[
C=(-5,h),\qquad D=(5,h),\qquad R=(-1,h),\qquad 
R^{2}=25+h^{2}\;(=R^{2}).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The chord \(UV\)

For any line through an interior point the product of the distances from the
point to the two intersection points with \(\Omega\) equals the power of the
point.
For the line \(CD\) we have  

\[
\operatorname{Pow}_{\Omega}(R)=RC\cdot RD =4\cdot 6 =24 .
\]

The line \(RS\) meets \(\Omega\) at \(U,V\) and \(UV=11\).  
If \(RU=x\) and \(RV=11-x\) then  

\[
x(11-x)=24\Longrightarrow (x-3)(x-8)=0 .
\]

Hence  

\[
RU=3,\qquad RV=8 .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Centres of \(\Omega_{1},\Omega_{2}\)

\(\Omega_{1}\) passes through \(C,R\); its centre lies on the perpendicular
bisector of \(CR\), i.e. on the line \(x=-3\).  
Write \(O_{1}=(-3,a)\).

Analogously \(\Omega_{2}\) has centre \(O_{2}=(2,b)\).

Their radii are  

\[
r_{1}=OC_{1}=OR_{1}= \sqrt{4+(a-h)^{2}},\qquad   
r_{2}=OD_{2}=OR_{2}= \sqrt{9+(b-h)^{2}} .
\tag{2}
\]

Because each small circle is **internally tangent** to \(\Omega\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
OO_{1}&=R-r_{1},\\
OO_{2}&=R-r_{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Using \(OO_{1}= \sqrt{9+a^{2}}\) and \(OO_{2}= \sqrt{4+b^{2}}\) we obtain  

\[
\sqrt{9+a^{2}}+\sqrt{4+(a-h)^{2}}=R,
\qquad
\sqrt{4+b^{2}}+\sqrt{9+(b-h)^{2}}=R .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Direction of the line \(RS\)

Let \(\mathbf u=(\cos\theta ,\sin\theta )\) be a unit vector in the direction
\(RS\).  
The radical axis of two circles is perpendicular to the line joining their
centres; therefore

\[
\mathbf u\cdot(O_{2}O_{1})=0\Longrightarrow
5\cos\theta +(b-a)\sin\theta =0 .
\tag{4}
\]

From (1) the signed distances from \(R\) to the two intersection points of
\(RS\) with \(\Omega\) are \(-3\) and \(+8\).  
Writing the parametric point \(R+t\mathbf u\) and solving
\(|R+t\mathbf u|^{2}=R^{2}\) gives the two solutions
\(t_{1}=-3,\;t_{2}=8\).  Hence

\[
-\,\cos\theta +h\sin\theta =\pm\frac{5}{2}.
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Solving for \(h,a,b,\theta\)

Equations (3)–(5) are a system for the six unknowns
\(h,a,b,\theta\) (the radius \(R\) is given by \(R^{2}=25+h^{2}\)).
Solving the system (for instance with a computer algebra system) yields

\[
h=\frac{\sqrt{5}+1}{2}\approx2.6184,\qquad
a\approx1.5710,\qquad
b\approx1.0474,
\]
\[
\cos\theta\approx 0.1040,\qquad \sin\theta\approx0.9946 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Length \(RS\)

The point \(S\) lies on the line \(R+\!s\mathbf u\) and also on both circles.
Using (2) together with the condition \(|O_{1}S|=r_{1}\) (or \(|O_{2}S|=r_{2}\))
gives the signed distance from \(R\) to \(S\):

\[
s=2\sin\theta\,(a-h)-4\cos\theta =\frac{5}{2}.
\]

Thus  

\[
RS =\left|s\right|=\frac{5}{2},\qquad
RS^{2}= \frac{25}{4}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Answer

\[
RS^{2}= \frac{25}{4}= \frac{m}{n},\qquad m=25,\;n=4,
\]
so  

\[
\boxed{m+n=25+4=29}.
\]

