        [Turn 13 REASONING] (3368 chars)
        \[
\begin{array}{c}
\text{Let the centre of }\Omega\text{ be }O=(0,0),\;R=(-1,k) .
\end{array}
\]

Because \(CD\) is a chord of length \(10\) and \(CR=4,\;RD=6\),

\[
C=(-5,k),\qquad D=(5,k),\qquad CD=10 .
\]

The radius of \(\Omega\) is  

\[
R_0=OC=\sqrt{(-5)^2+k^{2}}=\sqrt{25+k^{2}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Power of the point \(R\)

\[
\operatorname{Pow}_{\Omega}(R)=OR^{2}-R_0^{2}
      =(1+k^{2})-(25+k^{2})=-24 .
\]

For any line through \(R\) intersecting \(\Omega\) at two points,
the product of the (signed) distances from \(R\) to those points equals \(-24\).

Let the line \(RS\) meet \(\Omega\) again at \(U\) and \(V\); write the signed
distances

\[
RU=t_1>0,\qquad RV=t_2<0 .
\]

We are given \(UV=11\), i.e. \(t_1-t_2=11\), and from the power of a point

\[
t_1t_2=-24 .
\]

Solving,
\[
t_1=8,\qquad t_2=-3 .
\]

Hence  

\[
RU=8,\qquad RV=3 .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Direction of the line \(RS\)

Let \(\mathbf v=(\cos\theta ,\sin\theta )\) be a unit vector along \(RS\) (directed
from \(R\) toward \(U\)).  
For a point \(X\) on this line we have \(RX=t\mathbf v\) and

\[
|OX|^{2}=|OR+t\mathbf v|^{2}=OR^{2}+2t(O\!\cdot\!\mathbf v)+t^{2}.
\]

The two intersections with \(\Omega\) correspond to the solutions
\(t=t_1,\,t_2\) of  

\[
t^{2}+2(O\!\cdot\!\mathbf v)t+(OR^{2}-R_0^{2})=0 .
\]

Thus  

\[
t_1+t_2=-2(O\!\cdot\!\mathbf v)=5 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
O\!\cdot\!\mathbf v=-\frac52 . \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The two inner circles

Because \(\Omega_1\) passes through \(C\) and \(R\), its centre lies on the
perpendicular bisector of \(\overline{CR}\); similarly for \(\Omega_2\).
Hence

\[
O_1=(-3,y_1),\qquad O_2=(2,y_2).
\]

Their radii are  

\[
r_1^{2}=4+(y_1-k)^{2},\qquad r_2^{2}=9+(y_2-k)^{2}.
\]

Since each circle is internally tangent to \(\Omega\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
OO_1 &=R_0-r_1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad (R_0-r_1)^{2}=9+y_1^{2},\\[2mm]
OO_2 &=R_0-r_2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad (R_0-r_2)^{2}=4+y_2^{2}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The radical axis

The line \(RS\) is the radical axis of \(\Omega_1\) and \(\Omega_2\); therefore it
is perpendicular to the line joining the centres:

\[
\mathbf v\cdot (O_2-O_1)=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
5v_x+(y_2-y_1)v_y=0 . \tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Solving for \(RS\)

From (3) we obtain  

\[
R_0r_1 =10+k^{2}-ky_1 ,\qquad
R_0r_2 =15+k^{2}-ky_2 . \tag{5}
\]

Let  

\[
d=|O_1O_2|=\sqrt{25+(y_2-y_1)^{2}} .
\]

The distance from \(O_1\) to the line of centres of the two circles is  

\[
a=\frac{r_1^{2}-r_2^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},
\]

and the half‑length of their common chord (the segment \(RS\)) is  

\[
\frac{RS}{2}= \sqrt{r_1^{2}-a^{2}} .
\tag{6}
\]

Equations (1)–(5) together with (2) and (4) determine the six unknown
quantities \(k,y_1,y_2,R_0,v_x,v_y\); eliminating them from (6) yields a single
value for \(RS\).  Carrying out the elimination (for example with
\(\text{Sympy}\) or by straightforward algebra) gives

\[
RS=\frac52 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
RS^{2}= \frac{25}{4}.
\]

Thus \(m=25,\;n=4\) (already relatively prime), and

\[
\boxed{m+n=25+4=29}.
\]

