        [Turn 7 REASONING] (1685 chars)
        The three dice are rolled once.  
Kevin may keep exactly one die (the other two are re‑rolled) because \(n=2\).

For a kept die showing value \(k\) the two new dice must sum to  

\[
t = 7-k .
\]

The probability that two fair dice sum to \(t\) is  

\[
\frac{\#\{(x,y)\in\{1,\dots ,6\}^2:x+y=t\}}{36}
=
\begin{cases}
\frac{5}{36},&t=6\\[2pt]
\frac{4}{36},&t=5\\[2pt]
\frac{3}{36},&t=4\\[2pt]
\frac{2}{36},&t=3\\[2pt]
\frac{1}{36},&t=2\\[2pt]
0,&t\le 1\text{ or }t\ge 7 .
\end{cases}
\]

Since \(t=7-k\) and \(k\in\{1,\dots ,6\}\),

\[
k=1\Rightarrow t=6\;(5/36),\;
k=2\Rightarrow t=5\;(4/36),\;
k=3\Rightarrow t=4\;(3/36),\;
k=4\Rightarrow t=3\;(2/36),\;
k=5\Rightarrow t=2\;(1/36),\;
k=6\Rightarrow t=1\;(0).
\]

Thus the larger the probability the **smaller** the kept value.  
Hence, for any initial triple \((a,b,c)\) Kevin should keep the die with the
minimum value \(\min(a,b,c)\).  
The optimal probability for that triple is

\[
\frac{C(\min)}{36},\qquad
C(1)=5,\;C(2)=4,\;C(3)=3,\;C(4)=2,\;C(5)=1,\;C(6)=0 .
\]

---

### Counting triples by their minimum

Let \(N_m\) be the number of ordered triples whose minimum equals \(m\).

\[
N_m=(7-m)^3-(6-m)^3\quad (m=1,\dots ,5),\qquad N_6=1 .
\]

\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccc}
m & 1&2&3&4&5&6\\\hline
N_m&91&61&37&19&7&1
\end{array}
\]

---

### Total probability

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{\text{all triples}} C(\min)
&=5\cdot91+4\cdot61+3\cdot37+2\cdot19+1\cdot7+0\cdot1\\
&=855 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence the average (overall) probability is  

\[
p=\frac{1}{216}\cdot\frac{1}{36}\cdot 855
   =\frac{855}{7776}
   =\frac{95}{864}.
\]

Finally  

\[
216p = 216\cdot\frac{95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75 .
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{95}{4}}
\]

