        For each initial outcome \((a,b,c)\) Kevin must keep one die and re‑roll the other two.
If the kept die shows \(k\), the two re‑rolled dice have a sum \(S\) with  

\[
\Pr(S=s)=\frac{\#\{(x,y)\in\{1,\dots ,6\}^2:x+y=s\}}{36},
\qquad s=2,\dots ,12 .
\]

The numbers of ways to obtain each \(s\) are  

\[
1,2,3,4,5,6,5,4,3,2,1\quad (s=2,\dots ,12).
\]

Hence, for a kept value \(k\) the probability that the final total is \(7\) equals  

\[
\frac{\#\{(x,y):x+y=7-k\}}{36}.
\]

Define  

\[
f(k)=\#\{(x,y):x+y=7-k\}=
\begin{cases}
5&k=1\\
4&k=2\\
3&k=3\\
2&k=4\\
1&k=5\\
0&k=6 .
\end{cases}
\]

For a given initial triple \((a,b,c)\) Kevin will keep the die with the largest \(f\)-value, so the maximal success probability for that triple is  

\[
\frac{\max\{f(a),f(b),f(c)\}}{36}.
\]

Summing over all \(6^3=216\) possible initial triples gives  

\[
\sum_{a,b,c}\max\{f(a),f(b),f(c)\}=855 .
\]

Therefore the overall probability when \(n=2\) (i.e. exactly two dice are re‑rolled) is  

\[
p=\frac{1}{216}\cdot\frac{855}{36}
   =\frac{855}{7776}
   =\frac{95}{864}.
\]

Finally  

\[
216p = 216\cdot\frac{95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75.
\]

\[
