        For each first throw Kevin sees the three numbers \((a,b,c)\).
Since he must re‑roll exactly two dice, he will keep one die and re‑roll the other two.
If he keeps the die showing \(k\), the two new dice must sum to \(7-k\).

The ordered‑pair counts for a sum \(s\) of two dice are  

\[
\#(s)=\begin{cases}
1,2,3,4,5,6,5,4,3,2,1 & (s=2,3,\dots ,12)
\end{cases}
\]

Hence the probability of success when the kept die shows \(k\) is  

\[
f(k)=\frac{\#(7-k)}{36}=
\begin{cases}
\frac{5}{36}&k=1\\[2pt]
\frac{4}{36}&k=2\\[2pt]
\frac{3}{36}&k=3\\[2pt]
\frac{2}{36}&k=4\\[2pt]
\frac{1}{36}&k=5\\[2pt]
0 &k=6 .
\end{cases}
\]

To maximise the chance Kevin should keep the **smallest** of the three numbers,
because a smaller \(k\) gives a larger \(\#(7-k)\).

---

### Counting the contribution of each possible minimum

Let the minimum of \((a,b,c)\) be \(m\;(1\le m\le6)\).
The number of ordered triples whose minimum equals \(m\) is  

\[
(7-m)^3-(6-m)^3 .
\]

For such triples the maximal success probability is \(\frac{\#(7-m)}{36}\).

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
m & \text{triples with min}=m & \#(7-m) & \text{contribution to } \displaystyle\sum\max f(k)\\ \hline
1 & 6^3-5^3=91 & 5 & 91\cdot5=455\\
2 & 5^3-4^3=61 & 4 & 61\cdot4=244\\
3 & 4^3-3^3=37 & 3 & 37\cdot3=111\\
4 & 3^3-2^3=19 & 2 & 19\cdot2=38\\
5 & 2^3-1^3=7  & 1 & 7\cdot1=7\\
6 & 1^3-0   =1  & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\]

Adding the contributions gives  

\[
\sum_{\text{all triples}}\max f(k)=455+244+111+38+7=855 .
\]

---

### Probability and the requested value

For each of the \(216\) possible first throws the probability of ending with sum \(7\) is \(\displaystyle\frac{\max f(k)}{1}\).
Thus

\[
p=\frac{1}{216}\sum_{\text{triples}}\max f(k)
   =\frac{855}{216\cdot36}
   =\frac{95}{864}.
\]

Finally,

\[
216\,p = 216\cdot\frac{95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75 .
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{95}{4}}
\]

