        [Turn 9 REASONING] (1118 chars)
        The optimal strategy when exactly two dice may be re‑thrown is to keep the die showing the smallest value, because the probability of obtaining a required sum with the two re‑thrown dice is

\[
\Pr(X+Y = 7-v)=\frac{\#\{(x,y):x+y=7-v\}}{36},
\]

which is maximal when \(v\) is minimal.

For a given initial triple \((a,b,c)\) let \(m=\min\{a,b,c\}\).  
Then the optimal success probability for that triple is  

\[
\frac{c(7-m)}{36},
\qquad
c(7-m)=\begin{cases}
5,&m=1\\[2pt]
4,&m=2\\[2pt]
3,&m=3\\[2pt]
2,&m=4\\[2pt]
1,&m=5\\[2pt]
0,&m=6 .
\end{cases}
\]

Let \(N(m)\) be the number of ordered triples with minimum equal to \(m\):
\[
N(m)=(7-m)^3-(6-m)^3\qquad(m=1,\dots ,6).
\]

\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccc}
m &1&2&3&4&5&6\\\hline
N(m)&91&61&37&19&7&1
\end{array}
\]

Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
S&=\sum_{m=1}^{5} N(m)\,(6-m)\\
 &=91\cdot5+61\cdot4+37\cdot3+19\cdot2+7\cdot1\\
 &=855 .
\end{aligned}
\]

The overall probability of finishing with a sum of \(7\) is

\[
p=\frac{S}{216\cdot36}=\frac{855}{7776}=\frac{95}{864}.
\]

Finally

\[
216p=\frac{216\cdot95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75 .
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{95}{4}}
\]

